--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 928b0741cd687454781376cf5e0c26cc, type: 3}
  m_Name: AllVerseSO
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::AllVerseSO
  books:
  - bookID: 1
    book: Genesis
    passages:
    - passageID: 1001
      title: The Beginning
      pages:
      - pageID: 11001
        verses:
        - verse: 'In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth. '
        - verse: Now the earth was formless and empty, darkness was over the surface
            of the deep, and the Spirit of God was hovering over the waters.
      - pageID: 21001
        verses:
        - verse: "And God said, \u201CLet there be light,\u201D and there was light. "
      - pageID: 31001
        verses:
        - verse: God saw that the light was good, and he separated the light from
            the darkness.
        - verse: "God called the light \u201Cday,\u201D and the darkness he called
            \u201Cnight.\u201D And there was evening, and there was morning\u2014the
            first day."
      - pageID: 41001
        verses:
        - verse: "And God said, \u201CLet there be a vault between the waters to
            separate water from water.\u201D"
        - verse: So God made the vault and separated the water under the vault from
            the water above it. And it was so.
        - verse: "God called the vault \u201Csky.\u201D And there was evening, and
            there was morning\u2014the second day."
      - pageID: 51001
        verses:
        - verse: "And God said, \u201CLet the water under the sky be gathered to
            one place, and let dry ground appear.\u201D And it was so."
        - verse: "God called the dry ground \u201Cland,\u201D and the gathered waters
            he called \u201Cseas.\u201D And God saw that it was good."
      - pageID: 61001
        verses:
        - verse: "Then God said, \u201CLet the land produce vegetation: seed-bearing
            plants and trees on the land that bear fruit with seed in it, according
            to their various kinds.\u201D And it was so."
        - verse: 'The land produced vegetation: plants bearing seed according to
            their kinds and trees bearing fruit with seed in it according to their
            kinds. And God saw that it was good.'
        - verse: "And there was evening, and there was morning\u2014the third day."
      - pageID: 71001
        verses:
        - verse: "And God said, \u201CLet there be lights in the vault of the sky
            to separate the day from the night, and let them serve as signs to mark
            sacred times, and days and years,"
        - verse: "and let them be lights in the vault of the sky to give light on
            the earth.\u201D And it was so."
      - pageID: 81001
        verses:
        - verse: " God made two great lights\u2014the greater light to govern the
            day and the lesser light to govern the night. He also made the stars."
        - verse: God set them in the vault of the sky to give light on the earth,
        - verse: to govern the day and the night, and to separate light from darkness.
            And God saw that it was good.
      - pageID: 91001
        verses:
        - verse: "And there was evening, and there was morning\u2014the fourth day."
      - pageID: 101001
        verses:
        - verse: "And God said, \u201CLet the water teem with living creatures, and
            let birds fly above the earth across the vault of the sky.\u201D"
        - verse: So God created the great creatures of the sea and every living thing
            with which the water teems and that moves about in it, according to their
            kinds, and every winged bird according to its kind. And God saw that
            it was good.
      - pageID: 111001
        verses:
        - verse: "God blessed them and said, \u201CBe fruitful and increase in number
            and fill the water in the seas, and let the birds increase on the earth.\u201D"
        - verse: "And there was evening, and there was morning\u2014the fifth day."
      - pageID: 121001
        verses:
        - verse: "And God said, \u201CLet the land produce living creatures according
            to their kinds: the livestock, the creatures that move along the ground,
            and the wild animals, each according to its kind.\u201D And it was so."
        - verse: God made the wild animals according to their kinds, the livestock
            according to their kinds, and all the creatures that move along the ground
            according to their kinds. And God saw that it was good.
      - pageID: 131001
        verses:
        - verse: "Then God said, \u201CLet us make mankind in our image, in our likeness,
            so that they may rule over the fish in the sea and the birds in the sky,
            over the livestock and all the wild animals,[a] and over all the creatures
            that move along the ground.\u201D"
      - pageID: 141001
        verses:
        - verse: "So God created mankind in his own image,\r\n    in the image of
            God he created them;\r\n    male and female he created them."
      - pageID: 151001
        verses:
        - verse: "God blessed them and said to them, \u201CBe fruitful and increase
            in number; fill the earth and subdue it. Rule over the fish in the sea
            and the birds in the sky and over every living creature that moves on
            the ground.\u201D"
        - verse: "Then God said, \u201CI give you every seed-bearing plant on the
            face of the whole earth and every tree that has fruit with seed in it.
            They will be yours for food."
        - verse: "And to all the beasts of the earth and all the birds in the sky
            and all the creatures that move along the ground\u2014everything that
            has the breath of life in it\u2014I give every green plant for food.\u201D
            And it was so."
      - pageID: 161001
        verses:
        - verse: "God saw all that he had made, and it was very good. And there was
            evening, and there was morning\u2014the sixth day."
      - pageID: 171001
        verses:
        - verse: Thus the heavens and the earth were completed in all their vast
            array.
        - verse: By the seventh day God had finished the work he had been doing;
            so on the seventh day he rested from all his work.
        - verse: Then God blessed the seventh day and made it holy, because on it
            he rested from all the work of creating that he had done.
    - passageID: 1002
      title: Adam and Eve
      pages:
      - pageID: 11002
        verses:
        - verse: This is the account of the heavens and the earth when they were
            created, when the Lord God made the earth and the heavens.
        - verse: Now no shrub had yet appeared on the earth and no plant had yet
            sprung up, for the Lord God had not sent rain on the earth and there
            was no one to work the ground,
        - verse: but streams came up from the earth and watered the whole surface
            of the ground.
      - pageID: 21002
        verses:
        - verse: Then the Lord God formed a man from the dust of the ground and breathed
            into his nostrils the breath of life, and the man became a living being.
      - pageID: 31002
        verses:
        - verse: Now the Lord God had planted a garden in the east, in Eden; and
            there he put the man he had formed.
        - verse: "The Lord God made all kinds of trees grow out of the ground\u2014trees
            that were pleasing to the eye and good for food. In the middle of the
            garden were the tree of life and the tree of the knowledge of good and
            evil."
      - pageID: 41002
        verses:
        - verse: A river watering the garden flowed from Eden; from there it was
            separated into four headwaters.
        - verse: The name of the first is the Pishon; it winds through the entire
            land of Havilah, where there is gold.
        - verse: (The gold of that land is good; aromatic resin and onyx are also
            there.)
        - verse: The name of the second river is the Gihon; it winds through the
            entire land of Cush.
        - verse: The name of the third river is the Tigris; it runs along the east
            side of Ashur. And the fourth river is the Euphrates.
      - pageID: 51002
        verses:
        - verse: The Lord God took the man and put him in the Garden of Eden to work
            it and take care of it.
        - verse: "And the Lord God commanded the man, \u201CYou are free to eat from
            any tree in the garden;"
        - verse: "but you must not eat from the tree of the knowledge of good and
            evil, for when you eat from it you will certainly die.\u201D"
      - pageID: 61002
        verses:
        - verse: "The Lord God said, \u201CIt is not good for the man to be alone.
            I will make a helper suitable for him.\u201D"
      - pageID: 71002
        verses:
        - verse: Now the Lord God had formed out of the ground all the wild animals
            and all the birds in the sky. He brought them to the man to see what
            he would name them; and whatever the man called each living creature,
            that was its name.
        - verse: So the man gave names to all the livestock, the birds in the sky
            and all the wild animals. But for Adam no suitable helper was found.
      - pageID: 81002
        verses:
        - verse: "So the Lord God caused the man to fall into a deep sleep; and while
            he was sleeping, he took one of the man\u2019s ribs and then closed up
            the place with flesh."
        - verse: Then the Lord God made a woman from the rib he had taken out of
            the man, and he brought her to the man.
        - verse: "The man said,\r\n\r\n\u201CThis is now bone of my bones\r\n   
            and flesh of my flesh;\r\nshe shall be called \u2018woman,\u2019\r\n   
            for she was taken out of man.\u201D"
      - pageID: 91002
        verses:
        - verse: That is why a man leaves his father and mother and is united to
            his wife, and they become one flesh.
        - verse: Adam and his wife were both naked, and they felt no shame.
    - passageID: 1003
      title: The Fall
      pages:
      - pageID: 11003
        verses:
        - verse: "Now the serpent was more crafty than any of the wild animals the
            Lord God had made. He said to the woman, \u201CDid God really say, \u2018You
            must not eat from any tree in the garden\u2019?\u201D"
        - verse: "The woman said to the serpent, \u201CWe may eat fruit from the
            trees in the garden,"
        - verse: "but God did say, \u2018You must not eat fruit from the tree that
            is in the middle of the garden, and you must not touch it, or you will
            die.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 21003
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CYou will not certainly die,\u201D the serpent said to the
            woman."
        - verse: "\u201CFor God knows that when you eat from it your eyes will be
            opened, and you will be like God, knowing good and evil.\u201D"
      - pageID: 31003
        verses:
        - verse: When the woman saw that the fruit of the tree was good for food
            and pleasing to the eye, and also desirable for gaining wisdom, she took
            some and ate it. She also gave some to her husband, who was with her,
            and he ate it.
      - pageID: 41003
        verses:
        - verse: Then the eyes of both of them were opened, and they realized they
            were naked; so they sewed fig leaves together and made coverings for
            themselves.
        - verse: Then the man and his wife heard the sound of the Lord God as he
            was walking in the garden in the cool of the day, and they hid from the
            Lord God among the trees of the garden.
      - pageID: 51003
        verses:
        - verse: "But the Lord God called to the man, \u201CWhere are you?\u201D"
        - verse: "He answered, \u201CI heard you in the garden, and I was afraid
            because I was naked; so I hid.\u201D"
        - verse: "And he said, \u201CWho told you that you were naked? Have you eaten
            from the tree that I commanded you not to eat from?\u201D"
      - pageID: 61003
        verses:
        - verse: "The man said, \u201CThe woman you put here with me\u2014she gave
            me some fruit from the tree, and I ate it.\u201D"
        - verse: "Then the Lord God said to the woman, \u201CWhat is this you have
            done?\u201D The woman said, \u201CThe serpent deceived me, and I ate.\u201D"
      - pageID: 71003
        verses:
        - verse: "So the Lord God said to the serpent, \u201CBecause you have done
            this,\r\n\r\n\u201CCursed are you above all livestock\r\n    and all
            wild animals!\r\nYou will crawl on your belly\r\n    and you will eat
            dust\r\n    all the days of your life."
        - verse: "And I will put enmity\n    between you and the woman,\n    and
            between your offspring and hers;\nhe will crush your head,\n    and you
            will strike his heel.\u201D"
      - pageID: 81003
        verses:
        - verse: "To the woman he said,\r\n\r\n\u201CI will make your pains in childbearing
            very severe;\r\n    with painful labor you will give birth to children.\r\nYour
            desire will be for your husband,\r\n    and he will rule over you.\u201D"
      - pageID: 91003
        verses:
        - verse: "To Adam he said, \u201CBecause you listened to your wife and ate
            fruit from the tree about which I commanded you, \u2018You must not eat
            from it,\u2019\r\n\r\n\u201CCursed is the ground because of you;\r\n   
            through painful toil you will eat food from it\r\n    all the days of
            your life."
        - verse: "It will produce thorns and thistles for you,\r\n    and you will
            eat the plants of the field."
        - verse: "By the sweat of your brow\r\n    you will eat your food\r\nuntil
            you return to the ground,\r\n    since from it you were taken;\r\nfor
            dust you are\r\n    and to dust you will return.\u201D"
      - pageID: 101003
        verses:
        - verse: Adam named his wife Eve, because she would become the mother of
            all the living.
        - verse: The Lord God made garments of skin for Adam and his wife and clothed
            them.
      - pageID: 111003
        verses:
        - verse: "And the Lord God said, \u201CThe man has now become like one of
            us, knowing good and evil. He must not be allowed to reach out his hand
            and take also from the tree of life and eat, and live forever.\u201D"
        - verse: So the Lord God banished him from the Garden of Eden to work the
            ground from which he had been taken.
        - verse: After he drove the man out, he placed on the east side of the Garden
            of Eden cherubim and a flaming sword flashing back and forth to guard
            the way to the tree of life.
    - passageID: 1004
      title: Cain and Abel
      pages:
      - pageID: 11004
        verses:
        - verse: "Adam made love to his wife Eve, and she became pregnant and gave
            birth to Cain. She said, \u201CWith the help of the Lord I have brought
            forth a man.\u201D"
        - verse: Later she gave birth to his brother Abel. Now Abel kept flocks,
            and Cain worked the soil.
      - pageID: 21004
        verses:
        - verse: In the course of time Cain brought some of the fruits of the soil
            as an offering to the Lord.
        - verse: "And Abel also brought an offering\u2014fat portions from some of
            the firstborn of his flock. The Lord looked with favor on Abel and his
            offering,"
        - verse: but on Cain and his offering he did not look with favor. So Cain
            was very angry, and his face was downcast.
      - pageID: 31004
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Cain, \u201CWhy are you angry? Why is your
            face downcast?"
        - verse: "If you do what is right, will you not be accepted? But if you do
            not do what is right, sin is crouching at your door; it desires to have
            you, but you must rule over it.\u201D"
      - pageID: 41004
        verses:
        - verse: "Now Cain said to his brother Abel, \u201CLet\u2019s go out to the
            field.\u201D While they were in the field, Cain attacked his brother
            Abel and killed him."
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Cain, \u201CWhere is your brother Abel?\u201D\r\n\r\n\u201CI
            don\u2019t know,\u201D he replied. \u201CAm I my brother\u2019s keeper?\u201D"
        - verse: "The Lord said, \u201CWhat have you done? Listen! Your brother\u2019s
            blood cries out to me from the ground."
      - pageID: 51004
        verses:
        - verse: "Now you are under a curse and driven from the ground, which opened
            its mouth to receive your brother\u2019s blood from your hand."
        - verse: "When you work the ground, it will no longer yield its crops for
            you. You will be a restless wanderer on the earth.\u201D"
        - verse: "Cain said to the Lord, \u201CMy punishment is more than I can bear."
      - pageID: 61004
        verses:
        - verse: "Today you are driving me from the land, and I will be hidden from
            your presence; I will be a restless wanderer on the earth, and whoever
            finds me will kill me.\u201D"
        - verse: "But the Lord said to him, \u201CNot so; anyone who kills Cain will
            suffer vengeance seven times over.\u201D Then the Lord put a mark on
            Cain so that no one who found him would kill him."
        - verse: "So Cain went out from the Lord\u2019s presence and lived in the
            land of Nod, east of Eden."
      - pageID: 71004
        verses:
        - verse: Cain made love to his wife, and she became pregnant and gave birth
            to Enoch. Cain was then building a city, and he named it after his son
            Enoch.
        - verse: To Enoch was born Irad, and Irad was the father of Mehujael, and
            Mehujael was the father of Methushael, and Methushael was the father
            of Lamech.
        - verse: Lamech married two women, one named Adah and the other Zillah.
        - verse: Adah gave birth to Jabal; he was the father of those who live in
            tents and raise livestock.
        - verse: "His brother\u2019s name was Jubal; he was the father of all who
            play stringed instruments and pipes."
        - verse: "Zillah also had a son, Tubal-Cain, who forged all kinds of tools
            out of bronze and iron. Tubal-Cain\u2019s sister was Naamah."
      - pageID: 81004
        verses:
        - verse: "Lamech said to his wives,\r\n\r\n\u201CAdah and Zillah, listen
            to me;\r\n    wives of Lamech, hear my words.\r\nI have killed a man
            for wounding me,\r\n    a young man for injuring me."
        - verse: "If Cain is avenged seven times,\r\n    then Lamech seventy-seven
            times.\u201D"
      - pageID: 91004
        verses:
        - verse: "Adam made love to his wife again, and she gave birth to a son and
            named him Seth, saying, \u201CGod has granted me another child in place
            of Abel, since Cain killed him.\u201D"
        - verse: 'Seth also had a son, and he named him Enosh.


            At that
            time people began to call on the name of the Lord.'
    - passageID: 1005
      title: Wickedness in the World
      pages:
      - pageID: 11005
        verses:
        - verse: When human beings began to increase in number on the earth and daughters
            were born to them,
        - verse: the sons of God saw that the daughters of humans were beautiful,
            and they married any of them they chose.
      - pageID: 21005
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said, \u201CMy Spirit will not contend with humans
            forever, for they are mortal their days will be a hundred and twenty
            years.\u201D"
        - verse: "The Nephilim were on the earth in those days\u2014and also afterward\u2014when
            the sons of God went to the daughters of humans and had children by them.
            They were the heroes of old, men of renown."
      - pageID: 31005
        verses:
        - verse: The Lord saw how great the wickedness of the human race had become
            on the earth, and that every inclination of the thoughts of the human
            heart was only evil all the time.
        - verse: The Lord regretted that he had made human beings on the earth, and
            his heart was deeply troubled.
      - pageID: 41005
        verses:
        - verse: "So the Lord said, \u201CI will wipe from the face of the earth
            the human race I have created\u2014and with them the animals, the birds
            and the creatures that move along the ground\u2014for I regret that I
            have made them.\u201D"
        - verse: But Noah found favor in the eyes of the Lord.
    - passageID: 1006
      title: Noah and the Flood
      pages:
      - pageID: 41006
        verses:
        - verse: 'This is the account of Noah and his family.


            Noah
            was a righteous man, blameless among the people of his time, and he walked
            faithfully with God.'
      - pageID: 51006
        verses:
        - verse: 'Noah had three sons: Shem, Ham and Japheth.'
        - verse: "Now the earth was corrupt in God\u2019s sight and was full of violence."
        - verse: God saw how corrupt the earth had become, for all the people on
            earth had corrupted their ways.
      - pageID: 61006
        verses:
        - verse: "So God said to Noah, \u201CI am going to put an end to all people,
            for the earth is filled with violence because of them. I am surely going
            to destroy both them and the earth."
        - verse: 'So make yourself an ark of cypress wood; make rooms in it and coat
            it with pitch inside and out. '
      - pageID: 71006
        verses:
        - verse: 'This is how you are to build it: The ark is to be three hundred
            cubits long, fifty cubits wide and thirty cubits high.'
        - verse: Make a roof for it, leaving below the roof an opening one cubit
            high all around.Put a door in the side of the ark and make lower, middle
            and upper decks.
      - pageID: 81006
        verses:
        - verse: I am going to bring floodwaters on the earth to destroy all life
            under the heavens, every creature that has the breath of life in it.
            Everything on earth will perish.
        - verse: "But I will establish my covenant with you, and you will enter the
            ark\u2014you and your sons and your wife and your sons\u2019 wives with
            you."
      - pageID: 91006
        verses:
        - verse: You are to bring into the ark two of all living creatures, male
            and female, to keep them alive with you.
        - verse: Two of every kind of bird, of every kind of animal and of every
            kind of creature that moves along the ground will come to you to be kept
            alive.
        - verse: "You are to take every kind of food that is to be eaten and store
            it away as food for you and for them.\u201D"
        - verse: Noah did everything just as God commanded him.
    - passageID: 1007
      title: Noah and the Flood II
      pages:
      - pageID: 11007
        verses:
        - verse: "The Lord then said to Noah, \u201CGo into the ark, you and your
            whole family, because I have found you righteous in this generation."
        - verse: Take with you seven pairs of every kind of clean animal, a male
            and its mate, and one pair of every kind of unclean animal, a male and
            its mate,
        - verse: and also seven pairs of every kind of bird, male and female, to
            keep their various kinds alive throughout the earth.
      - pageID: 21007
        verses:
        - verse: "Seven days from now I will send rain on the earth for forty days
            and forty nights, and I will wipe from the face of the earth every living
            creature I have made.\u201D"
        - verse: And Noah did all that the Lord commanded him.
        - verse: Noah was six hundred years old when the floodwaters came on the
            earth.
        - verse: "And Noah and his sons and his wife and his sons\u2019 wives entered
            the ark to escape the waters of the flood."
      - pageID: 31007
        verses:
        - verse: Pairs of clean and unclean animals, of birds and of all creatures
            that move along the ground,
        - verse: male and female, came to Noah and entered the ark, as God had commanded
            Noah.
        - verse: And after the seven days the floodwaters came on the earth.
      - pageID: 41007
        verses:
        - verse: "In the six hundredth year of Noah\u2019s life, on the seventeenth
            day of the second month\u2014on that day all the springs of the great
            deep burst forth, and the floodgates of the heavens were opened."
        - verse: And rain fell on the earth forty days and forty nights.
      - pageID: 51007
        verses:
        - verse: 'On that very day Noah and his sons, Shem, Ham and Japheth, together
            with his wife and the wives of his three sons, entered the ark. '
      - pageID: 61007
        verses:
        - verse: They had with them every wild animal according to its kind, all
            livestock according to their kinds, every creature that moves along the
            ground according to its kind and every bird according to its kind, everything
            with wings.
        - verse: Pairs of all creatures that have the breath of life in them came
            to Noah and entered the ark.
        - verse: The animals going in were male and female of every living thing,
            as God had commanded Noah. Then the Lord shut him in.
      - pageID: 71007
        verses:
        - verse: For forty days the flood kept coming on the earth, and as the waters
            increased they lifted the ark high above the earth.
        - verse: The waters rose and increased greatly on the earth, and the ark
            floated on the surface of the water.
        - verse: They rose greatly on the earth, and all the high mountains under
            the entire heavens were covered
        - verse: The waters rose and covered the mountains to a depth of more than
            fifteen cubits.
      - pageID: 81007
        verses:
        - verse: "Every living thing that moved on land perished\u2014birds, livestock,
            wild animals, all the creatures that swarm over the earth, and all mankind."
        - verse: Everything on dry land that had the breath of life in its nostrils
            died.
        - verse: Every living thing on the face of the earth was wiped out; people
            and animals and the creatures that move along the ground and the birds
            were wiped from the earth. Only Noah was left, and those with him in
            the ark.
      - pageID: 91007
        verses:
        - verse: The waters flooded the earth for a hundred and fifty days.
    - passageID: 1008
      title: Noah and the Flood III
      pages:
      - pageID: 11008
        verses:
        - verse: But God remembered Noah and all the wild animals and the livestock
            that were with him in the ark, and he sent a wind over the earth, and
            the waters receded.
        - verse: Now the springs of the deep and the floodgates of the heavens had
            been closed, and the rain had stopped falling from the sky.
        - verse: The water receded steadily from the earth. At the end of the hundred
            and fifty days the water had gone down,
        - verse: and on the seventeenth day of the seventh month the ark came to
            rest on the mountains of Ararat.
      - pageID: 21008
        verses:
        - verse: The waters continued to recede until the tenth month, and on the
            first day of the tenth month the tops of the mountains became visible.
        - verse: After forty days Noah opened a window he had made in the ark
        - verse: and sent out a raven, and it kept flying back and forth until the
            water had dried up from the earth.
        - verse: Then he sent out a dove to see if the water had receded from the
            surface of the ground.
      - pageID: 31008
        verses:
        - verse: But the dove could find nowhere to perch because there was water
            over all the surface of the earth; so it returned to Noah in the ark.
            He reached out his hand and took the dove and brought it back to himself
            in the ark.
        - verse: He waited seven more days and again sent out the dove from the ark.
        - verse: When the dove returned to him in the evening, there in its beak
            was a freshly plucked olive leaf! Then Noah knew that the water had receded
            from the earth.
      - pageID: 41008
        verses:
        - verse: He waited seven more days and sent the dove out again, but this
            time it did not return to him.
        - verse: "By the first day of the first month of Noah\u2019s six hundred
            and first year, the water had dried up from the earth. Noah then removed
            the covering from the ark and saw that the surface of the ground was
            dry."
        - verse: By the twenty-seventh day of the second month the earth was completely
            dry.
      - pageID: 51008
        verses:
        - verse: Then God said to Noah,
        - verse: "\u201CCome out of the ark, you and your wife and your sons and
            their wives."
        - verse: "Bring out every kind of living creature that is with you\u2014the
            birds, the animals, and all the creatures that move along the ground\u2014so
            they can multiply on the earth and be fruitful and increase in number
            on it.\u201D"
      - pageID: 61008
        verses:
        - verse: "So Noah came out, together with his sons and his wife and his sons\u2019
            wives."
        - verse: "All the animals and all the creatures that move along the ground
            and all the birds\u2014everything that moves on land\u2014came out of
            the ark, one kind after another."
      - pageID: 71008
        verses:
        - verse: Then Noah built an altar to the Lord and, taking some of all the
            clean animals and clean birds, he sacrificed burnt offerings on it.
        - verse: "The Lord smelled the pleasing aroma and said in his heart: \u201CNever
            again will I curse the ground because of humans, even though every inclination
            of the human heart is evil from childhood. And never again will I destroy
            all living creatures, as I have done."
      - pageID: 81008
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CAs long as the earth endures,\r\nseedtime and harvest,\r\ncold
            and heat,\r\nsummer and winter,\r\nday and night\r\nwill never cease.\u201D"
    - passageID: 1009
      title: God's Covenant with Noah
      pages:
      - pageID: 11009
        verses:
        - verse: "Then God blessed Noah and his sons, saying to them, \u201CBe fruitful
            and increase in number and fill the earth."
        - verse: The fear and dread of you will fall on all the beasts of the earth,
            and on all the birds in the sky, on every creature that moves along the
            ground, and on all the fish in the sea; they are given into your hands.
      - pageID: 21009
        verses:
        - verse: Everything that lives and moves about will be food for you. Just
            as I gave you the green plants, I now give you everything.
        - verse: "\u201CBut you must not eat meat that has its lifeblood still in
            it."
        - verse: And for your lifeblood I will surely demand an accounting. I will
            demand an accounting from every animal. And from each human being, too,
            I will demand an accounting for the life of another human being.
      - pageID: 31009
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CWhoever sheds human blood,\r\n    by humans shall their blood
            be shed;\r\nfor in the image of God\r\n    has God made mankind."
        - verse: "As for you, be fruitful and increase in number; multiply on the
            earth and increase upon it.\u201D"
        - verse: 'Then God said to Noah and to his sons with him:'
      - pageID: 41009
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CI now establish my covenant with you and with your descendants
            after you"
        - verse: "and with every living creature that was with you\u2014the birds,
            the livestock and all the wild animals, all those that came out of the
            ark with you\u2014every living creature on earth."
      - pageID: 51009
        verses:
        - verse: "I establish my covenant with you: Never again will all life be
            destroyed by the waters of a flood; never again will there be a flood
            to destroy the earth.\u201D"
        - verse: "And God said, \u201CThis is the sign of the covenant I am making
            between me and you and every living creature with you, a covenant for
            all generations to come:"
      - pageID: 61009
        verses:
        - verse: I have set my rainbow in the clouds, and it will be the sign of
            the covenant between me and the earth.
        - verse: Whenever I bring clouds over the earth and the rainbow appears in
            the clouds,
      - pageID: 71009
        verses:
        - verse: I will remember my covenant between me and you and all living creatures
            of every kind. Never again will the waters become a flood to destroy
            all life.
        - verse: "Whenever the rainbow appears in the clouds, I will see it and remember
            the everlasting covenant between God and all living creatures of every
            kind on the earth.\u201D"
      - pageID: 81009
        verses:
        - verse: "So God said to Noah, \u201CThis is the sign of the covenant I have
            established between me and all life on the earth.\u201D"
    - passageID: 1010
      title: The Tower of Babel
      pages:
      - pageID: 11010
        verses:
        - verse: Now the whole world had one language and a common speech.
        - verse: As people moved eastward, they found a plain in Shinar and settled
            there.
        - verse: "They said to each other, \u201CCome, let\u2019s make bricks and
            bake them thoroughly.\u201D They used brick instead of stone, and tar
            for mortar."
      - pageID: 21010
        verses:
        - verse: "Then they said, \u201CCome, let us build ourselves a city, with
            a tower that reaches to the heavens, so that we may make a name for ourselves;
            otherwise we will be scattered over the face of the whole earth.\u201D"
        - verse: But the Lord came down to see the city and the tower the people
            were building.
        - verse: "The Lord said, \u201CIf as one people speaking the same language
            they have begun to do this, then nothing they plan to do will be impossible
            for them."
      - pageID: 31010
        verses:
        - verse: "Come, let us go down and confuse their language so they will not
            understand each other.\u201D"
        - verse: So the Lord scattered them from there over all the earth, and they
            stopped building the city.
      - pageID: 41010
        verses:
        - verse: "That is why it was called Babel\u2014because there the Lord confused
            the language of the whole world. From there the Lord scattered them over
            the face of the whole earth."
    - passageID: 1011
      title: The Call of Abram
      pages:
      - pageID: 11011
        verses:
        - verse: "The Lord had said to Abram, \u201CGo from your country, your people
            and your father\u2019s household to the land I will show you."
      - pageID: 21011
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CI will make you into a great nation,\n    and I will bless
            you;\nI will make your name great,\n    and you will be a blessing."
        - verse: "I will bless those who bless you,\r\n    and whoever curses you
            I will curse;\r\nand all peoples on earth\r\n    will be blessed through
            you.\u201D"
      - pageID: 31011
        verses:
        - verse: So Abram went, as the Lord had told him; and Lot went with him.
            Abram was seventy-five years old when he set out from Harran.
        - verse: He took his wife Sarai, his nephew Lot, all the possessions they
            had accumulated and the people they had acquired in Harran, and they
            set out for the land of Canaan, and they arrived there.
      - pageID: 41011
        verses:
        - verse: Abram traveled through the land as far as the site of the great
            tree of Moreh at Shechem. At that time the Canaanites were in the land.
        - verse: "The Lord appeared to Abram and said, \u201CTo your offspring I
            will give this land.\u201D So he built an altar there to the Lord, who
            had appeared to him."
      - pageID: 51011
        verses:
        - verse: From there he went on toward the hills east of Bethel and pitched
            his tent, with Bethel on the west and Ai on the east. There he built
            an altar to the Lord and called on the name of the Lord.
        - verse: Then Abram set out and continued toward the Negev.
    - passageID: 1012
      title: Abram in Egypt
      pages:
      - pageID: 11012
        verses:
        - verse: Now there was a famine in the land, and Abram went down to Egypt
            to live there for a while because the famine was severe.
        - verse: "As he was about to enter Egypt, he said to his wife Sarai, \u201CI
            know what a beautiful woman you are."
      - pageID: 21012
        verses:
        - verse: "When the Egyptians see you, they will say, \u2018This is his wife.\u2019
            Then they will kill me but will let you live."
        - verse: "Say you are my sister, so that I will be treated well for your
            sake and my life will be spared because of you.\u201D"
      - pageID: 31012
        verses:
        - verse: When Abram came to Egypt, the Egyptians saw that Sarai was a very
            beautiful woman.
        - verse: "And when Pharaoh\u2019s officials saw her, they praised her to
            Pharaoh, and she was taken into his palace."
        - verse: He treated Abram well for her sake, and Abram acquired sheep and
            cattle, male and female donkeys, male and female servants, and camels.
      - pageID: 41012
        verses:
        - verse: "But the Lord inflicted serious diseases on Pharaoh and his household
            because of Abram\u2019s wife Sarai."
        - verse: "So Pharaoh summoned Abram. \u201CWhat have you done to me?\u201D
            he said. \u201CWhy didn\u2019t you tell me she was your wife?"
      - pageID: 51012
        verses:
        - verse: "Why did you say, \u2018She is my sister,\u2019 so that I took her
            to be my wife? Now then, here is your wife. Take her and go!\u201D"
        - verse: Then Pharaoh gave orders about Abram to his men, and they sent him
            on his way, with his wife and everything he had.
    - passageID: 1013
      title: Abram and Lot Separate
      pages:
      - pageID: 11013
        verses:
        - verse: So Abram went up from Egypt to the Negev, with his wife and everything
            he had, and Lot went with him.
        - verse: Abram had become very wealthy in livestock and in silver and gold.
      - pageID: 21013
        verses:
        - verse: From the Negev he went from place to place until he came to Bethel,
            to the place between Bethel and Ai where his tent had been earlier
        - verse: and where he had first built an altar. There Abram called on the
            name of the Lord.
      - pageID: 31013
        verses:
        - verse: Now Lot, who was moving about with Abram, also had flocks and herds
            and tents.
        - verse: But the land could not support them while they stayed together,
            for their possessions were so great that they were not able to stay together.
        - verse: "And quarreling arose between Abram\u2019s herders and Lot\u2019s.
            The Canaanites and Perizzites were also living in the land at that time."
      - pageID: 41013
        verses:
        - verse: "So Abram said to Lot, \u201CLet\u2019s not have any quarreling
            between you and me, or between your herders and mine, for we are close
            relatives."
        - verse: "Is not the whole land before you? Let\u2019s part company. If you
            go to the left, I\u2019ll go to the right; if you go to the right, I\u2019ll
            go to the left.\u201D"
      - pageID: 51013
        verses:
        - verse: Lot looked around and saw that the whole plain of the Jordan toward
            Zoar was well watered, like the garden of the Lord, like the land of
            Egypt. (This was before the Lord destroyed Sodom and Gomorrah.)
      - pageID: 61013
        verses:
        - verse: 'So Lot chose for himself the whole plain of the Jordan and set
            out toward the east. The two men parted company:'
        - verse: Abram lived in the land of Canaan, while Lot lived among the cities
            of the plain and pitched his tents near Sodom.
      - pageID: 71013
        verses:
        - verse: Now the people of Sodom were wicked and were sinning greatly against
            the Lord.
      - pageID: 81013
        verses:
        - verse: "The Lord said to Abram after Lot had parted from him, \u201CLook
            around from where you are, to the north and south, to the east and west."
        - verse: All the land that you see I will give to you and your offspring
            forever.
        - verse: I will make your offspring like the dust of the earth, so that if
            anyone could count the dust, then your offspring could be counted.
      - pageID: 91013
        verses:
        - verse: "Go, walk through the length and breadth of the land, for I am giving
            it to you.\u201D"
        - verse: So Abram went to live near the great trees of Mamre at Hebron, where
            he pitched his tents. There he built an altar to the Lord.
    - passageID: 1014
      title: Abram Rescues Lot
      pages:
      - pageID: 11014
        verses:
        - verse: At the time when Amraphel was king of Shinar, Arioch king of Ellasar,
            Kedorlaomer king of Elam and Tidal king of Goyim,
        - verse: these kings went to war against Bera king of Sodom, Birsha king
            of Gomorrah, Shinab king of Admah, Shemeber king of Zeboyim, and the
            king of Bela (that is, Zoar).
        - verse: All these latter kings joined forces in the Valley of Siddim (that
            is, the Dead Sea Valley).
      - pageID: 21014
        verses:
        - verse: For twelve years they had been subject to Kedorlaomer, but in the
            thirteenth year they rebelled.
        - verse: In the fourteenth year, Kedorlaomer and the kings allied with him
            went out and defeated the Rephaites in Ashteroth Karnaim, the Zuzites
            in Ham, the Emites in Shaveh Kiriathaim
        - verse: and the Horites in the hill country of Seir, as far as El Paran
            near the desert.
      - pageID: 31014
        verses:
        - verse: Then they turned back and went to En Mishpat (that is, Kadesh),
            and they conquered the whole territory of the Amalekites, as well as
            the Amorites who were living in Hazezon Tamar.
        - verse: Then the king of Sodom, the king of Gomorrah, the king of Admah,
            the king of Zeboyim and the king of Bela (that is, Zoar) marched out
            and drew up their battle lines in the Valley of Siddim
        - verse: "against Kedorlaomer king of Elam, Tidal king of Goyim, Amraphel
            king of Shinar and Arioch king of Ellasar\u2014four kings against five."
      - pageID: 41014
        verses:
        - verse: Now the Valley of Siddim was full of tar pits, and when the kings
            of Sodom and Gomorrah fled, some of the men fell into them and the rest
            fled to the hills.
        - verse: The four kings seized all the goods of Sodom and Gomorrah and all
            their food; then they went away.
        - verse: "They also carried off Abram\u2019s nephew Lot and his possessions,
            since he was living in Sodom."
      - pageID: 51014
        verses:
        - verse: A man who had escaped came and reported this to Abram the Hebrew.
            Now Abram was living near the great trees of Mamre the Amorite, a brother
            of Eshkol and Aner, all of whom were allied with Abram.
        - verse: When Abram heard that his relative had been taken captive, he called
            out the 318 trained men born in his household and went in pursuit as
            far as Dan.
      - pageID: 61014
        verses:
        - verse: During the night Abram divided his men to attack them and he routed
            them, pursuing them as far as Hobah, north of Damascus.
        - verse: He recovered all the goods and brought back his relative Lot and
            his possessions, together with the women and the other people.
      - pageID: 71014
        verses:
        - verse: "After Abram returned from defeating Kedorlaomer and the kings allied
            with him, the king of Sodom came out to meet him in the Valley of Shaveh
            (that is, the King\u2019s Valley)."
        - verse: Then Melchizedek king of Salem brought out bread and wine. He was
            priest of God Most High,
      - pageID: 81014
        verses:
        - verse: "and he blessed Abram, saying,\r\n\r\n\u201CBlessed be Abram by
            God Most High,\r\n    Creator of heaven and earth."
        - verse: "And praise be to God Most High,\r\n    who delivered your enemies
            into your hand.\u201D\r\n\r\nThen Abram gave him a tenth of everything."
      - pageID: 91014
        verses:
        - verse: "The king of Sodom said to Abram, \u201CGive me the people and keep
            the goods for yourself.\u201D"
        - verse: "But Abram said to the king of Sodom, \u201CWith raised hand I have
            sworn an oath to the Lord, God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth,"
      - pageID: 101014
        verses:
        - verse: "that I will accept nothing belonging to you, not even a thread
            or the strap of a sandal, so that you will never be able to say, \u2018I
            made Abram rich.\u2019"
        - verse: "I will accept nothing but what my men have eaten and the share
            that belongs to the men who went with me\u2014to Aner, Eshkol and Mamre.
            Let them have their share.\u201D"
    - passageID: 1015
      title: The Lord's Covenant with Abram
      pages:
      - pageID: 11015
        verses:
        - verse: "After this, the word of the Lord came to Abram in a vision:\r\n\r\n\u201CDo
            not be afraid, Abram.\r\n    I am your shield,\r\n    your very great
            reward.\u201D"
        - verse: "But Abram said, \u201CSovereign Lord, what can you give me since
            I remain childless and the one who will inherit my estate is Eliezer
            of Damascus?\u201D"
        - verse: "And Abram said, \u201CYou have given me no children; so a servant
            in my household will be my heir.\u201D"
      - pageID: 21015
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the word of the Lord came to him: \u201CThis man will not
            be your heir, but a son who is your own flesh and blood will be your
            heir.\u201D"
        - verse: "He took him outside and said, \u201CLook up at the sky and count
            the stars\u2014if indeed you can count them.\u201D Then he said to him,
            \u201CSo shall your offspring be.\u201D"
        - verse: Abram believed the Lord, and he credited it to him as righteousness.
      - pageID: 31015
        verses:
        - verse: "He also said to him, \u201CI am the Lord, who brought you out of
            Ur of the Chaldeans to give you this land to take possession of it.\u201D"
        - verse: "But Abram said, \u201CSovereign Lord, how can I know that I will
            gain possession of it?\u201D"
        - verse: "So the Lord said to him, \u201CBring me a heifer, a goat and a
            ram, each three years old, along with a dove and a young pigeon.\u201D"
      - pageID: 41015
        verses:
        - verse: Abram brought all these to him, cut them in two and arranged the
            halves opposite each other; the birds, however, he did not cut in half.
        - verse: Then birds of prey came down on the carcasses, but Abram drove them
            away.
        - verse: As the sun was setting, Abram fell into a deep sleep, and a thick
            and dreadful darkness came over him.
      - pageID: 51015
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to him, \u201CKnow for certain that for four
            hundred years your descendants will be strangers in a country not their
            own and that they will be enslaved and mistreated there."
        - verse: But I will punish the nation they serve as slaves, and afterward
            they will come out with great possessions.
        - verse: You, however, will go to your ancestors in peace and be buried at
            a good old age.
        - verse: "In the fourth generation your descendants will come back here,
            for the sin of the Amorites has not yet reached its full measure.\u201D"
      - pageID: 61015
        verses:
        - verse: When the sun had set and darkness had fallen, a smoking firepot
            with a blazing torch appeared and passed between the pieces.
        - verse: "On that day the Lord made a covenant with Abram and said, \u201CTo
            your descendants I give this land, from the Wadi of Egypt to the great
            river, the Euphrates\u2014"
        - verse: the land of the Kenites, Kenizzites, Kadmonites,
        - verse: Hittites, Perizzites, Rephaites,
        - verse: "Amorites, Canaanites, Girgashites and Jebusites.\u201D"
    - passageID: 1016
      title: Joseph's Dreams
      pages:
      - pageID: 11016
        verses:
        - verse: Jacob lived in the land where his father had stayed, the land of
            Canaan.
        - verse: "This is the account of Jacob\u2019s family line.\r\n\r\nJoseph,
            a young man of seventeen, was tending the flocks with his brothers, the
            sons of Bilhah and the sons of Zilpah, his father\u2019s wives, and he
            brought their father a bad report about them."
      - pageID: 21016
        verses:
        - verse: Now Israel loved Joseph more than any of his other sons, because
            he had been born to him in his old age; and he made an ornate robe for
            him.
        - verse: When his brothers saw that their father loved him more than any
            of them, they hated him and could not speak a kind word to him.
        - verse: Joseph had a dream, and when he told it to his brothers, they hated
            him all the more.
      - pageID: 31016
        verses:
        - verse: "He said to them, \u201CListen to this dream I had:"
        - verse: "We were binding sheaves of grain out in the field when suddenly
            my sheaf rose and stood upright, while your sheaves gathered around mine
            and bowed down to it.\u201D"
        - verse: "His brothers said to him, \u201CDo you intend to reign over us?
            Will you actually rule us?\u201D And they hated him all the more because
            of his dream and what he had said."
      - pageID: 41016
        verses:
        - verse: "Then he had another dream, and he told it to his brothers. \u201CListen,\u201D
            he said, \u201CI had another dream, and this time the sun and moon and
            eleven stars were bowing down to me.\u201D"
        - verse: "When he told his father as well as his brothers, his father rebuked
            him and said, \u201CWhat is this dream you had? Will your mother and
            I and your brothers actually come and bow down to the ground before you?\u201D"
        - verse: His brothers were jealous of him, but his father kept the matter
            in mind.
    - passageID: 1017
      title: Joseph Sold by His Brothers
      pages:
      - pageID: 11017
        verses:
        - verse: "Now his brothers had gone to graze their father\u2019s flocks near
            Shechem,"
        - verse: "and Israel said to Joseph, \u201CAs you know, your brothers are
            grazing the flocks near Shechem. Come, I am going to send you to them.\u201D\r\n\r\n\u201CVery
            well,\u201D he replied."
      - pageID: 21017
        verses:
        - verse: "So he said to him, \u201CGo and see if all is well with your brothers
            and with the flocks, and bring word back to me.\u201D Then he sent him
            off from the Valley of Hebron.\r\n\r\nWhen Joseph arrived at Shechem,"
        - verse: "a man found him wandering around in the fields and asked him, \u201CWhat
            are you looking for?\u201D"
        - verse: "He replied, \u201CI\u2019m looking for my brothers. Can you tell
            me where they are grazing their flocks?\u201D"
      - pageID: 31017
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CThey have moved on from here,\u201D the man answered. \u201CI
            heard them say, \u2018Let\u2019s go to Dothan.\u2019\u201D\r\n\r\nSo
            Joseph went after his brothers and found them near Dothan."
        - verse: But they saw him in the distance, and before he reached them, they
            plotted to kill him.
        - verse: "\u201CHere comes that dreamer!\u201D they said to each other."
      - pageID: 41017
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CCome now, let\u2019s kill him and throw him into one of these
            cisterns and say that a ferocious animal devoured him. Then we\u2019ll
            see what comes of his dreams.\u201D"
        - verse: "When Reuben heard this, he tried to rescue him from their hands.
            \u201CLet\u2019s not take his life,\u201D he said."
        - verse: "\u201CDon\u2019t shed any blood. Throw him into this cistern here
            in the wilderness, but don\u2019t lay a hand on him.\u201D Reuben said
            this to rescue him from them and take him back to his father."
      - pageID: 51017
        verses:
        - verse: "So when Joseph came to his brothers, they stripped him of his robe\u2014the
            ornate robe he was wearing\u2014"
        - verse: and they took him and threw him into the cistern. The cistern was
            empty; there was no water in it.
      - pageID: 61017
        verses:
        - verse: As they sat down to eat their meal, they looked up and saw a caravan
            of Ishmaelites coming from Gilead. Their camels were loaded with spices,
            balm and myrrh, and they were on their way to take them down to Egypt.
      - pageID: 71017
        verses:
        - verse: "Judah said to his brothers, \u201CWhat will we gain if we kill
            our brother and cover up his blood?"
        - verse: "Come, let\u2019s sell him to the Ishmaelites and not lay our hands
            on him; after all, he is our brother, our own flesh and blood.\u201D
            His brothers agreed."
      - pageID: 81017
        verses:
        - verse: So when the Midianite merchants came by, his brothers pulled Joseph
            up out of the cistern and sold him for twenty shekels of silver to the
            Ishmaelites, who took him to Egypt.
      - pageID: 91017
        verses:
        - verse: When Reuben returned to the cistern and saw that Joseph was not
            there, he tore his clothes.
        - verse: "He went back to his brothers and said, \u201CThe boy isn\u2019t
            there! Where can I turn now?\u201D"
        - verse: "Then they got Joseph\u2019s robe, slaughtered a goat and dipped
            the robe in the blood."
      - pageID: 101017
        verses:
        - verse: "They took the ornate robe back to their father and said, \u201CWe
            found this. Examine it to see whether it is your son\u2019s robe.\u201D"
        - verse: "He recognized it and said, \u201CIt is my son\u2019s robe! Some
            ferocious animal has devoured him. Joseph has surely been torn to pieces.\u201D"
      - pageID: 111017
        verses:
        - verse: Then Jacob tore his clothes, put on sackcloth and mourned for his
            son many days.
        - verse: "All his sons and daughters came to comfort him, but he refused
            to be comforted. \u201CNo,\u201D he said, \u201CI will continue to mourn
            until I join my son in the grave.\u201D So his father wept for him."
        - verse: "Meanwhile, the Midianites sold Joseph in Egypt to Potiphar, one
            of Pharaoh\u2019s officials, the captain of the guard."
    - passageID: 1018
      title: Joseph and Potiphar's Wife
      pages:
      - pageID: 11018
        verses:
        - verse: "Now Joseph had been taken down to Egypt. Potiphar, an Egyptian
            who was one of Pharaoh\u2019s officials, the captain of the guard, bought
            him from the Ishmaelites who had taken him there."
        - verse: The Lord was with Joseph so that he prospered, and he lived in the
            house of his Egyptian master.
        - verse: When his master saw that the Lord was with him and that the Lord
            gave him success in everything he did,
      - pageID: 21018
        verses:
        - verse: Joseph found favor in his eyes and became his attendant. Potiphar
            put him in charge of his household, and he entrusted to his care everything
            he owned.
        - verse: From the time he put him in charge of his household and of all that
            he owned, the Lord blessed the household of the Egyptian because of Joseph.
            The blessing of the Lord was on everything Potiphar had, both in the
            house and in the field.
      - pageID: 31018
        verses:
        - verse: "So Potiphar left everything he had in Joseph\u2019s care; with
            Joseph in charge, he did not concern himself with anything except the
            food he ate. Now Joseph was well-built and handsome,"
        - verse: "and after a while his master\u2019s wife took notice of Joseph
            and said, \u201CCome to bed with me!\u201D"
      - pageID: 41018
        verses:
        - verse: "But he refused. \u201CWith me in charge,\u201D he told her, \u201Cmy
            master does not concern himself with anything in the house; everything
            he owns he has entrusted to my care."
        - verse: "No one is greater in this house than I am. My master has withheld
            nothing from me except you, because you are his wife. How then could
            I do such a wicked thing and sin against God?\u201D"
        - verse: And though she spoke to Joseph day after day, he refused to go to
            bed with her or even be with her.
      - pageID: 51018
        verses:
        - verse: One day he went into the house to attend to his duties, and none
            of the household servants was inside.
        - verse: "She caught him by his cloak and said, \u201CCome to bed with me!\u201D
            But he left his cloak in her hand and ran out of the house."
        - verse: When she saw that he had left his cloak in her hand and had run
            out of the house,
      - pageID: 61018
        verses:
        - verse: "she called her household servants. \u201CLook,\u201D she said to
            them, \u201Cthis Hebrew has been brought to us to make sport of us! He
            came in here to sleep with me, but I screamed."
        - verse: "When he heard me scream for help, he left his cloak beside me and
            ran out of the house.\u201D"
        - verse: She kept his cloak beside her until his master came home.
      - pageID: 71018
        verses:
        - verse: "Then she told him this story: \u201CThat Hebrew slave you brought
            us came to me to make sport of me."
        - verse: "But as soon as I screamed for help, he left his cloak beside me
            and ran out of the house.\u201D"
        - verse: "When his master heard the story his wife told him, saying, \u201CThis
            is how your slave treated me,\u201D he burned with anger."
        - verse: "Joseph\u2019s master took him and put him in prison, the place
            where the king\u2019s prisoners were confined. But while Joseph was there
            in the prison,"
      - pageID: 81018
        verses:
        - verse: the Lord was with him; he showed him kindness and granted him favor
            in the eyes of the prison warden.
        - verse: So the warden put Joseph in charge of all those held in the prison,
            and he was made responsible for all that was done there.
      - pageID: 91018
        verses:
        - verse: "The warden paid no attention to anything under Joseph\u2019s care,
            because the Lord was with Joseph and gave him success in whatever he
            did."
    - passageID: 1019
      title: The Cupbearer and the Baker
      pages:
      - pageID: 11019
        verses:
        - verse: Some time later, the cupbearer and the baker of the king of Egypt
            offended their master, the king of Egypt.
        - verse: Pharaoh was angry with his two officials, the chief cupbearer and
            the chief baker,
        - verse: and put them in custody in the house of the captain of the guard,
            in the same prison where Joseph was confined.
      - pageID: 21019
        verses:
        - verse: 'The captain of the guard assigned them to Joseph, and he attended
            them.



            After they had been in custody for some time,'
        - verse: "each of the two men\u2014the cupbearer and the baker of the king
            of Egypt, who were being held in prison\u2014had a dream the same night,
            and each dream had a meaning of its own."
      - pageID: 31019
        verses:
        - verse: When Joseph came to them the next morning, he saw that they were
            dejected.
        - verse: "So he asked Pharaoh\u2019s officials who were in custody with him
            in his master\u2019s house, \u201CWhy do you look so sad today?\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CWe both had dreams,\u201D they answered, \u201Cbut there
            is no one to interpret them.\u201D\r\n\r\nThen Joseph said to them, \u201CDo
            not interpretations belong to God? Tell me your dreams.\u201D"
      - pageID: 41019
        verses:
        - verse: "So the chief cupbearer told Joseph his dream. He said to him, \u201CIn
            my dream I saw a vine in front of me,"
        - verse: and on the vine were three branches. As soon as it budded, it blossomed,
            and its clusters ripened into grapes.
      - pageID: 51019
        verses:
        - verse: "Pharaoh\u2019s cup was in my hand, and I took the grapes, squeezed
            them into Pharaoh\u2019s cup and put the cup in his hand.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CThis is what it means,\u201D Joseph said to him. \u201CThe
            three branches are three days."
      - pageID: 61019
        verses:
        - verse: "Within three days Pharaoh will lift up your head and restore you
            to your position, and you will put Pharaoh\u2019s cup in his hand, just
            as you used to do when you were his cupbearer."
        - verse: But when all goes well with you, remember me and show me kindness;
            mention me to Pharaoh and get me out of this prison.
        - verse: "I was forcibly carried off from the land of the Hebrews, and even
            here I have done nothing to deserve being put in a dungeon.\u201D"
      - pageID: 71019
        verses:
        - verse: "When the chief baker saw that Joseph had given a favorable interpretation,
            he said to Joseph, \u201CI too had a dream: On my head were three baskets
            of bread."
        - verse: "In the top basket were all kinds of baked goods for Pharaoh, but
            the birds were eating them out of the basket on my head.\u201D"
      - pageID: 81019
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CThis is what it means,\u201D Joseph said. \u201CThe three
            baskets are three days."
        - verse: "Within three days Pharaoh will lift off your head and impale your
            body on a pole. And the birds will eat away your flesh.\u201D"
      - pageID: 91019
        verses:
        - verse: "Now the third day was Pharaoh\u2019s birthday, and he gave a feast
            for all his officials. He lifted up the heads of the chief cupbearer
            and the chief baker in the presence of his officials:"
      - pageID: 101019
        verses:
        - verse: "He restored the chief cupbearer to his position, so that he once
            again put the cup into Pharaoh\u2019s hand\u2014"
        - verse: but he impaled the chief baker, just as Joseph had said to them
            in his interpretation.
        - verse: The chief cupbearer, however, did not remember Joseph; he forgot
            him.
    - passageID: 1020
      title: Pharaoh's Dreams
      pages:
      - pageID: 11020
        verses:
        - verse: 'When two full years had passed, Pharaoh had a dream: He was standing
            by the Nile,'
        - verse: when out of the river there came up seven cows, sleek and fat, and
            they grazed among the reeds.
      - pageID: 21020
        verses:
        - verse: After them, seven other cows, ugly and gaunt, came up out of the
            Nile and stood beside those on the riverbank.
        - verse: And the cows that were ugly and gaunt ate up the seven sleek, fat
            cows. Then Pharaoh woke up.
        - verse: 'He fell asleep again and had a second dream: Seven heads of grain,
            healthy and good, were growing on a single stalk.'
      - pageID: 31020
        verses:
        - verse: "After them, seven other heads of grain sprouted\u2014thin and scorched
            by the east wind."
        - verse: The thin heads of grain swallowed up the seven healthy, full heads.
            Then Pharaoh woke up; it had been a dream.
      - pageID: 41020
        verses:
        - verse: In the morning his mind was troubled, so he sent for all the magicians
            and wise men of Egypt. Pharaoh told them his dreams, but no one could
            interpret them for him.
        - verse: "Then the chief cupbearer said to Pharaoh, \u201CToday I am reminded
            of my shortcomings."
        - verse: Pharaoh was once angry with his servants, and he imprisoned me and
            the chief baker in the house of the captain of the guard.
      - pageID: 51020
        verses:
        - verse: Each of us had a dream the same night, and each dream had a meaning
            of its own.
        - verse: Now a young Hebrew was there with us, a servant of the captain of
            the guard. We told him our dreams, and he interpreted them for us, giving
            each man the interpretation of his dream.
        - verse: "And things turned out exactly as he interpreted them to us: I was
            restored to my position, and the other man was impaled.\u201D"
      - pageID: 61020
        verses:
        - verse: ' So Pharaoh sent for Joseph, and he was quickly brought from the
            dungeon. When he had shaved and changed his clothes, he came before Pharaoh.'
        - verse: "Pharaoh said to Joseph, \u201CI had a dream, and no one can interpret
            it. But I have heard it said of you that when you hear a dream you can
            interpret it.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CI cannot do it,\u201D Joseph replied to Pharaoh, \u201Cbut
            God will give Pharaoh the answer he desires.\u201D"
      - pageID: 71020
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh said to Joseph, \u201CIn my dream I was standing on
            the bank of the Nile,"
        - verse: when out of the river there came up seven cows, fat and sleek, and
            they grazed among the reeds.
        - verse: "After them, seven other cows came up\u2014scrawny and very ugly
            and lean. I had never seen such ugly cows in all the land of Egypt."
        - verse: The lean, ugly cows ate up the seven fat cows that came up first.
      - pageID: 81020
        verses:
        - verse: But even after they ate them, no one could tell that they had done
            so; they looked just as ugly as before. Then I woke up.
        - verse: "\u201CIn my dream I saw seven heads of grain, full and good, growing
            on a single stalk."
        - verse: "After them, seven other heads sprouted\u2014withered and thin and
            scorched by the east wind."
        - verse: "The thin heads of grain swallowed up the seven good heads. I told
            this to the magicians, but none of them could explain it to me.\u201D"
      - pageID: 91020
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Joseph said to Pharaoh, \u201CThe dreams of Pharaoh are one
            and the same. God has revealed to Pharaoh what he is about to do."
        - verse: The seven good cows are seven years, and the seven good heads of
            grain are seven years; it is one and the same dream.
      - pageID: 101020
        verses:
        - verse: 'The seven lean, ugly cows that came up afterward are seven years,
            and so are the seven worthless heads of grain scorched by the east wind:
            They are seven years of famine.'
      - pageID: 111020
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CIt is just as I said to Pharaoh: God has shown Pharaoh what
            he is about to do."
        - verse: Seven years of great abundance are coming throughout the land of
            Egypt,
        - verse: but seven years of famine will follow them. Then all the abundance
            in Egypt will be forgotten, and the famine will ravage the land.
      - pageID: 121020
        verses:
        - verse: The abundance in the land will not be remembered, because the famine
            that follows it will be so severe.
        - verse: The reason the dream was given to Pharaoh in two forms is that the
            matter has been firmly decided by God, and God will do it soon.
        - verse: "\u201CAnd now let Pharaoh look for a discerning and wise man and
            put him in charge of the land of Egypt."
      - pageID: 131020
        verses:
        - verse: Let Pharaoh appoint commissioners over the land to take a fifth
            of the harvest of Egypt during the seven years of abundance.
        - verse: They should collect all the food of these good years that are coming
            and store up the grain under the authority of Pharaoh, to be kept in
            the cities for food.
        - verse: "This food should be held in reserve for the country, to be used
            during the seven years of famine that will come upon Egypt, so that the
            country may not be ruined by the famine.\u201D"
      - pageID: 141020
        verses:
        - verse: The plan seemed good to Pharaoh and to all his officials.
        - verse: "So Pharaoh asked them, \u201CCan we find anyone like this man,
            one in whom is the spirit of God?"
      - pageID: 151020
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh said to Joseph, \u201CSince God has made all this
            known to you, there is no one so discerning and wise as you."
        - verse: "You shall be in charge of my palace, and all my people are to submit
            to your orders. Only with respect to the throne will I be greater than
            you.\u201D"
    - passageID: 1021
      title: Joseph in Charge of Egypt
      pages:
      - pageID: 11021
        verses:
        - verse: "So Pharaoh said to Joseph, \u201CI hereby put you in charge of
            the whole land of Egypt.\u201D"
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh took his signet ring from his finger and put it on
            Joseph\u2019s finger. He dressed him in robes of fine linen and put a
            gold chain around his neck."
        - verse: "He had him ride in a chariot as his second-in-command, and people
            shouted before him, \u201CMake way!\u201D Thus he put him in charge of
            the whole land of Egypt."
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh said to Joseph, \u201CI am Pharaoh, but without your
            word no one will lift hand or foot in all Egypt.\u201D"
      - pageID: 21021
        verses:
        - verse: Pharaoh gave Joseph the name Zaphenath-Paneah and gave him Asenath
            daughter of Potiphera, priest of On, to be his wife. And Joseph went
            throughout the land of Egypt.
        - verse: "Joseph was thirty years old when he entered the service of Pharaoh
            king of Egypt. And Joseph went out from Pharaoh\u2019s presence and traveled
            throughout Egypt."
        - verse: During the seven years of abundance the land produced plentifully.
      - pageID: 31021
        verses:
        - verse: Joseph collected all the food produced in those seven years of abundance
            in Egypt and stored it in the cities. In each city he put the food grown
            in the fields surrounding it.
        - verse: Joseph stored up huge quantities of grain, like the sand of the
            sea; it was so much that he stopped keeping records because it was beyond
            measure.
      - pageID: 41021
        verses:
        - verse: Before the years of famine came, two sons were born to Joseph by
            Asenath daughter of Potiphera, priest of On.
        - verse: "Joseph named his firstborn Manasseh and said, \u201CIt is because
            God has made me forget all my trouble and all my father\u2019s household.\u201D"
        - verse: "The second son he named Ephraim and said, \u201CIt is because God
            has made me fruitful in the land of my suffering.\u201D"
      - pageID: 51021
        verses:
        - verse: The seven years of abundance in Egypt came to an end,
        - verse: and the seven years of famine began, just as Joseph had said. There
            was famine in all the other lands, but in the whole land of Egypt there
            was food.
      - pageID: 61021
        verses:
        - verse: "When all Egypt began to feel the famine, the people cried to Pharaoh
            for food. Then Pharaoh told all the Egyptians, \u201CGo to Joseph and
            do what he tells you.\u201D"
        - verse: When the famine had spread over the whole country, Joseph opened
            all the storehouses and sold grain to the Egyptians, for the famine was
            severe throughout Egypt.
        - verse: And all the world came to Egypt to buy grain from Joseph, because
            the famine was severe everywhere.
    - passageID: 1022
      title: Joseph's Brothers Go to Egypt
      pages:
      - pageID: 11022
        verses:
        - verse: "When Jacob learned that there was grain in Egypt, he said to his
            sons, \u201CWhy do you just keep looking at each other?\u201D"
        - verse: "He continued, \u201CI have heard that there is grain in Egypt.
            Go down there and buy some for us, so that we may live and not die.\u201D"
      - pageID: 21022
        verses:
        - verse: "Then ten of Joseph\u2019s brothers went down to buy grain from
            Egypt."
        - verse: "But Jacob did not send Benjamin, Joseph\u2019s brother, with the
            others, because he was afraid that harm might come to him."
        - verse: "So Israel\u2019s sons were among those who went to buy grain, for
            there was famine in the land of Canaan also."
      - pageID: 31022
        verses:
        - verse: "Now Joseph was the governor of the land, the person who sold grain
            to all its people. So when Joseph\u2019s brothers arrived, they bowed
            down to him with their faces to the ground."
        - verse: "As soon as Joseph saw his brothers, he recognized them, but he
            pretended to be a stranger and spoke harshly to them. \u201CWhere do
            you come from?\u201D he asked.\r\n\r\n\u201CFrom the land of Canaan,\u201D
            they replied, \u201Cto buy food.\u201D"
        - verse: Although Joseph recognized his brothers, they did not recognize
            him.
      - pageID: 41022
        verses:
        - verse: "Then he remembered his dreams about them and said to them, \u201CYou
            are spies! You have come to see where our land is unprotected.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CNo, my lord,\u201D they answered. \u201CYour servants have
            come to buy food."
        - verse: "We are all the sons of one man. Your servants are honest men, not
            spies.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CNo!\u201D he said to them. \u201CYou have come to see where
            our land is unprotected.\u201D"
      - pageID: 51022
        verses:
        - verse: "But they replied, \u201CYour servants were twelve brothers, the
            sons of one man, who lives in the land of Canaan. The youngest is now
            with our father, and one is no more.\u201D"
        - verse: "Joseph said to them, \u201CIt is just as I told you: You are spies!"
      - pageID: 61022
        verses:
        - verse: 'And this is how you will be tested: As surely as Pharaoh lives,
            you will not leave this place unless your youngest brother comes here.'
        - verse: "Send one of your number to get your brother; the rest of you will
            be kept in prison, so that your words may be tested to see if you are
            telling the truth. If you are not, then as surely as Pharaoh lives, you
            are spies!\u201D"
        - verse: And he put them all in custody for three days.
      - pageID: 71022
        verses:
        - verse: "On the third day, Joseph said to them, \u201CDo this and you will
            live, for I fear God:"
        - verse: If you are honest men, let one of your brothers stay here in prison,
            while the rest of you go and take grain back for your starving households.
        - verse: "But you must bring your youngest brother to me, so that your words
            may be verified and that you may not die.\u201D This they proceeded to
            do."
      - pageID: 81022
        verses:
        - verse: "They said to one another, \u201CSurely we are being punished because
            of our brother. We saw how distressed he was when he pleaded with us
            for his life, but we would not listen; that\u2019s why this distress
            has come on us.\u201D"
        - verse: "Reuben replied, \u201CDidn\u2019t I tell you not to sin against
            the boy? But you wouldn\u2019t listen! Now we must give an accounting
            for his blood.\u201D"
        - verse: They did not realize that Joseph could understand them, since he
            was using an interpreter.
      - pageID: 91022
        verses:
        - verse: He turned away from them and began to weep, but then came back and
            spoke to them again. He had Simeon taken from them and bound before their
            eyes.
        - verse: "Joseph gave orders to fill their bags with grain, to put each man\u2019s
            silver back in his sack, and to give them provisions for their journey.
            After this was done for them,"
        - verse: they loaded their grain on their donkeys and left.
      - pageID: 101022
        verses:
        - verse: At the place where they stopped for the night one of them opened
            his sack to get feed for his donkey, and he saw his silver in the mouth
            of his sack.
        - verse: "\u201CMy silver has been returned,\u201D he said to his brothers.
            \u201CHere it is in my sack.\u201D\r\n\r\nTheir hearts sank and they
            turned to each other trembling and said, \u201CWhat is this that God
            has done to us?\u201D"
      - pageID: 111022
        verses:
        - verse: When they came to their father Jacob in the land of Canaan, they
            told him all that had happened to them. They said,
        - verse: "\u201CThe man who is lord over the land spoke harshly to us and
            treated us as though we were spying on the land."
        - verse: "But we said to him, \u2018We are honest men; we are not spies."
        - verse: "We were twelve brothers, sons of one father. One is no more, and
            the youngest is now with our father in Canaan.\u2019"
      - pageID: 121022
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CThen the man who is lord over the land said to us, \u2018This
            is how I will know whether you are honest men: Leave one of your brothers
            here with me, and take food for your starving households and go."
        - verse: "But bring your youngest brother to me so I will know that you are
            not spies but honest men. Then I will give your brother back to you,
            and you can trade in the land.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 131022
        verses:
        - verse: "As they were emptying their sacks, there in each man\u2019s sack
            was his pouch of silver! When they and their father saw the money pouches,
            they were frightened."
        - verse: "Their father Jacob said to them, \u201CYou have deprived me of
            my children. Joseph is no more and Simeon is no more, and now you want
            to take Benjamin. Everything is against me!\u201D"
      - pageID: 141022
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Reuben said to his father, \u201CYou may put both of my sons
            to death if I do not bring him back to you. Entrust him to my care, and
            I will bring him back.\u201D"
        - verse: "But Jacob said, \u201CMy son will not go down there with you; his
            brother is dead and he is the only one left. If harm comes to him on
            the journey you are taking, you will bring my gray head down to the grave
            in sorrow.\u201D"
    - passageID: 1023
      title: The Second Journey to Egypt
      pages:
      - pageID: 11023
        verses:
        - verse: Now the famine was still severe in the land.
        - verse: "So when they had eaten all the grain they had brought from Egypt,
            their father said to them, \u201CGo back and buy us a little more food.\u201D"
      - pageID: 21023
        verses:
        - verse: "But Judah said to him, \u201CThe man warned us solemnly, \u2018You
            will not see my face again unless your brother is with you.\u2019"
        - verse: If you will send our brother along with us, we will go down and
            buy food for you.
        - verse: "But if you will not send him, we will not go down, because the
            man said to us, \u2018You will not see my face again unless your brother
            is with you.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 31023
        verses:
        - verse: "Israel asked, \u201CWhy did you bring this trouble on me by telling
            the man you had another brother?\u201D"
        - verse: "They replied, \u201CThe man questioned us closely about ourselves
            and our family. \u2018Is your father still living?\u2019 he asked us.
            \u2018Do you have another brother?\u2019 We simply answered his questions.
            How were we to know he would say, \u2018Bring your brother down here\u2019?\u201D"
        - verse: "Then Judah said to Israel his father, \u201CSend the boy along
            with me and we will go at once, so that we and you and our children may
            live and not die."
      - pageID: 41023
        verses:
        - verse: I myself will guarantee his safety; you can hold me personally responsible
            for him. If I do not bring him back to you and set him here before you,
            I will bear the blame before you all my life.
        - verse: "As it is, if we had not delayed, we could have gone and returned
            twice.\u201D"
        - verse: "Then their father Israel said to them, \u201CIf it must be, then
            do this: Put some of the best products of the land in your bags and take
            them down to the man as a gift\u2014a little balm and a little honey,
            some spices and myrrh, some pistachio nuts and almonds."
      - pageID: 51023
        verses:
        - verse: Take double the amount of silver with you, for you must return the
            silver that was put back into the mouths of your sacks. Perhaps it was
            a mistake.
        - verse: Take your brother also and go back to the man at once.
        - verse: "And may God Almighty grant you mercy before the man so that he
            will let your other brother and Benjamin come back with you. As for me,
            if I am bereaved, I am bereaved.\u201D"
      - pageID: 61023
        verses:
        - verse: So the men took the gifts and double the amount of silver, and Benjamin
            also. They hurried down to Egypt and presented themselves to Joseph.
        - verse: "When Joseph saw Benjamin with them, he said to the steward of his
            house, \u201CTake these men to my house, slaughter an animal and prepare
            a meal; they are to eat with me at noon.\u201D"
        - verse: "The man did as Joseph told him and took the men to Joseph\u2019s
            house."
      - pageID: 71023
        verses:
        - verse: "Now the men were frightened when they were taken to his house.
            They thought, \u201CWe were brought here because of the silver that was
            put back into our sacks the first time. He wants to attack us and overpower
            us and seize us as slaves and take our donkeys.\u201D"
      - pageID: 81023
        verses:
        - verse: "So they went up to Joseph\u2019s steward and spoke to him at the
            entrance to the house."
        - verse: "\u201CWe beg your pardon, our lord,\u201D they said, \u201Cwe came
            down here the first time to buy food."
        - verse: "But at the place where we stopped for the night we opened our sacks
            and each of us found his silver\u2014the exact weight\u2014in the mouth
            of his sack. So we have brought it back with us."
        - verse: "We have also brought additional silver with us to buy food. We
            don\u2019t know who put our silver in our sacks.\u201D"
      - pageID: 91023
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CIt\u2019s all right,\u201D he said. \u201CDon\u2019t be afraid.
            Your God, the God of your father, has given you treasure in your sacks;
            I received your silver.\u201D Then he brought Simeon out to them."
        - verse: "The steward took the men into Joseph\u2019s house, gave them water
            to wash their feet and provided fodder for their donkeys."
        - verse: "They prepared their gifts for Joseph\u2019s arrival at noon, because
            they had heard that they were to eat there."
      - pageID: 101023
        verses:
        - verse: When Joseph came home, they presented to him the gifts they had
            brought into the house, and they bowed down before him to the ground.
        - verse: "He asked them how they were, and then he said, \u201CHow is your
            aged father you told me about? Is he still living?\u201D"
        - verse: "They replied, \u201CYour servant our father is still alive and
            well.\u201D And they bowed down, prostrating themselves before him."
      - pageID: 111023
        verses:
        - verse: "As he looked about and saw his brother Benjamin, his own mother\u2019s
            son, he asked, \u201CIs this your youngest brother, the one you told
            me about?\u201D And he said, \u201CGod be gracious to you, my son.\u201D"
        - verse: Deeply moved at the sight of his brother, Joseph hurried out and
            looked for a place to weep. He went into his private room and wept there.
      - pageID: 121023
        verses:
        - verse: "After he had washed his face, he came out and, controlling himself,
            said, \u201CServe the food.\u201D"
        - verse: They served him by himself, the brothers by themselves, and the
            Egyptians who ate with him by themselves, because Egyptians could not
            eat with Hebrews, for that is detestable to Egyptians.
      - pageID: 131023
        verses:
        - verse: The men had been seated before him in the order of their ages, from
            the firstborn to the youngest; and they looked at each other in astonishment.
        - verse: "When portions were served to them from Joseph\u2019s table, Benjamin\u2019s
            portion was five times as much as anyone else\u2019s. So they feasted
            and drank freely with him."
    - passageID: 1024
      title: A Silver Cup in a Sack
      pages:
      - pageID: 11024
        verses:
        - verse: "Now Joseph gave these instructions to the steward of his house:
            \u201CFill the men\u2019s sacks with as much food as they can carry,
            and put each man\u2019s silver in the mouth of his sack."
        - verse: "Then put my cup, the silver one, in the mouth of the youngest one\u2019s
            sack, along with the silver for his grain.\u201D And he did as Joseph
            said."
      - pageID: 21024
        verses:
        - verse: As morning dawned, the men were sent on their way with their donkeys.
        - verse: "They had not gone far from the city when Joseph said to his steward,
            \u201CGo after those men at once, and when you catch up with them, say
            to them, \u2018Why have you repaid good with evil?"
        - verse: "Isn\u2019t this the cup my master drinks from and also uses for
            divination? This is a wicked thing you have done.\u2019\u201D"
        - verse: When he caught up with them, he repeated these words to them.
      - pageID: 31024
        verses:
        - verse: "But they said to him, \u201CWhy does my lord say such things? Far
            be it from your servants to do anything like that!"
        - verse: "We even brought back to you from the land of Canaan the silver
            we found inside the mouths of our sacks. So why would we steal silver
            or gold from your master\u2019s house?"
        - verse: "If any of your servants is found to have it, he will die; and the
            rest of us will become my lord\u2019s slaves.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CVery well, then,\u201D he said, \u201Clet it be as you say.
            Whoever is found to have it will become my slave; the rest of you will
            be free from blame.\u201D"
      - pageID: 41024
        verses:
        - verse: Each of them quickly lowered his sack to the ground and opened it.
        - verse: "Then the steward proceeded to search, beginning with the oldest
            and ending with the youngest. And the cup was found in Benjamin\u2019s
            sack."
        - verse: At this, they tore their clothes. Then they all loaded their donkeys
            and returned to the city.
      - pageID: 51024
        verses:
        - verse: Joseph was still in the house when Judah and his brothers came in,
            and they threw themselves to the ground before him.
        - verse: "Joseph said to them, \u201CWhat is this you have done? Don\u2019t
            you know that a man like me can find things out by divination?\u201D"
      - pageID: 61024
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CWhat can we say to my lord?\u201D Judah replied. \u201CWhat
            can we say? How can we prove our innocence? God has uncovered your servants\u2019
            guilt. We are now my lord\u2019s slaves\u2014we ourselves and the one
            who was found to have the cup.\u201D"
        - verse: "But Joseph said, \u201CFar be it from me to do such a thing! Only
            the man who was found to have the cup will become my slave. The rest
            of you, go back to your father in peace.\u201D"
      - pageID: 71024
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Judah went up to him and said: \u201CPardon your servant,
            my lord, let me speak a word to my lord. Do not be angry with your servant,
            though you are equal to Pharaoh himself."
        - verse: "My lord asked his servants, \u2018Do you have a father or a brother?\u2019"
        - verse: "And we answered, \u2018We have an aged father, and there is a young
            son born to him in his old age. His brother is dead, and he is the only
            one of his mother\u2019s sons left, and his father loves him.\u2019"
      - pageID: 81024
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CThen you said to your servants, \u2018Bring him down to me
            so I can see him for myself.\u2019"
        - verse: "And we said to my lord, \u2018The boy cannot leave his father;
            if he leaves him, his father will die.\u2019"
        - verse: "But you told your servants, \u2018Unless your youngest brother
            comes down with you, you will not see my face again.\u2019"
      - pageID: 91024
        verses:
        - verse: When we went back to your servant my father, we told him what my
            lord had said.
        - verse: " \u201CThen our father said, \u2018Go back and buy a little more
            food.\u2019"
        - verse: "But we said, \u2018We cannot go down. Only if our youngest brother
            is with us will we go. We cannot see the man\u2019s face unless our youngest
            brother is with us.\u2019"
      - pageID: 101024
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CYour servant my father said to us, \u2018You know that my
            wife bore me two sons."
        - verse: "One of them went away from me, and I said, \u201CHe has surely
            been torn to pieces.\u201D And I have not seen him since. "
        - verse: "If you take this one from me too and harm comes to him, you will
            bring my gray head down to the grave in misery.\u2019"
      - pageID: 111024
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CSo now, if the boy is not with us when I go back to your
            servant my father, and if my father, whose life is closely bound up with
            the boy\u2019s life,"
        - verse: "sees that the boy isn\u2019t there, he will die. Your servants
            will bring the gray head of our father down to the grave in sorrow."
      - pageID: 121024
        verses:
        - verse: "Your servant guaranteed the boy\u2019s safety to my father. I said,
            \u2018If I do not bring him back to you, I will bear the blame before
            you, my father, all my life!\u2019"
        - verse: "\u201CNow then, please let your servant remain here as my lord\u2019s
            slave in place of the boy, and let the boy return with his brothers."
        - verse: "How can I go back to my father if the boy is not with me? No! Do
            not let me see the misery that would come on my father.\u201D"
    - passageID: 1025
      title: Joseph Makes Himself Known
      pages:
      - pageID: 11025
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Joseph could no longer control himself before all his attendants,
            and he cried out, \u201CHave everyone leave my presence!\u201D So there
            was no one with Joseph when he made himself known to his brothers."
        - verse: "And he wept so loudly that the Egyptians heard him, and Pharaoh\u2019s
            household heard about it."
      - pageID: 21025
        verses:
        - verse: "Joseph said to his brothers, \u201CI am Joseph! Is my father still
            living?\u201D But his brothers were not able to answer him, because they
            were terrified at his presence.\r\n\r"
        - verse: "Then Joseph said to his brothers, \u201CCome close to me.\u201D
            When they had done so, he said, \u201CI am your brother Joseph, the one
            you sold into Egypt!"
        - verse: And now, do not be distressed and do not be angry with yourselves
            for selling me here, because it was to save lives that God sent me ahead
            of you.
      - pageID: 31025
        verses:
        - verse: For two years now there has been famine in the land, and for the
            next five years there will be no plowing and reaping.
        - verse: But God sent me ahead of you to preserve for you a remnant on earth
            and to save your lives by a great deliverance.
      - pageID: 41025
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CSo then, it was not you who sent me here, but God. He made
            me father to Pharaoh, lord of his entire household and ruler of all Egypt."
        - verse: "Now hurry back to my father and say to him, \u2018This is what
            your son Joseph says: God has made me lord of all Egypt. Come down to
            me; don\u2019t delay."
        - verse: "You shall live in the region of Goshen and be near me\u2014you,
            your children and grandchildren, your flocks and herds, and all you have."
      - pageID: 51025
        verses:
        - verse: "I will provide for you there, because five years of famine are
            still to come. Otherwise you and your household and all who belong to
            you will become destitute.\u2019"
        - verse: "\u201CYou can see for yourselves, and so can my brother Benjamin,
            that it is really I who am speaking to you."
        - verse: "Tell my father about all the honor accorded me in Egypt and about
            everything you have seen. And bring my father down here quickly.\u201D"
      - pageID: 61025
        verses:
        - verse: Then he threw his arms around his brother Benjamin and wept, and
            Benjamin embraced him, weeping.
        - verse: And he kissed all his brothers and wept over them. Afterward his
            brothers talked with him.
        - verse: "When the news reached Pharaoh\u2019s palace that Joseph\u2019s
            brothers had come, Pharaoh and all his officials were pleased."
      - pageID: 71025
        verses:
        - verse: "Pharaoh said to Joseph, \u201CTell your brothers, \u2018Do this:
            Load your animals and return to the land of Canaan,"
        - verse: "and bring your father and your families back to me. I will give
            you the best of the land of Egypt and you can enjoy the fat of the land.\u2019"
      - pageID: 81025
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CYou are also directed to tell them, \u2018Do this: Take some
            carts from Egypt for your children and your wives, and get your father
            and come."
        - verse: "Never mind about your belongings, because the best of all Egypt
            will be yours.\u2019\u201D"
        - verse: So the sons of Israel did this. Joseph gave them carts, as Pharaoh
            had commanded, and he also gave them provisions for their journey.
      - pageID: 91025
        verses:
        - verse: To each of them he gave new clothing, but to Benjamin he gave three
            hundred shekels of silver and five sets of clothes.
        - verse: 'And this is what he sent to his father: ten donkeys loaded with
            the best things of Egypt, and ten female donkeys loaded with grain and
            bread and other provisions for his journey.'
        - verse: "Then he sent his brothers away, and as they were leaving he said
            to them, \u201CDon\u2019t quarrel on the way!\u201D"
      - pageID: 101025
        verses:
        - verse: So they went up out of Egypt and came to their father Jacob in the
            land of Canaan.
        - verse: "They told him, \u201CJoseph is still alive! In fact, he is ruler
            of all Egypt.\u201D Jacob was stunned; he did not believe them."
      - pageID: 111025
        verses:
        - verse: But when they told him everything Joseph had said to them, and when
            he saw the carts Joseph had sent to carry him back, the spirit of their
            father Jacob revived.
        - verse: "And Israel said, \u201CI\u2019m convinced! My son Joseph is still
            alive. I will go and see him before I die.\u201D"
  - bookID: 2
    book: Exodus
    passages:
    - passageID: 2001
      title: The Israelites Oppressed
      pages:
      - pageID: 12001
        verses:
        - verse: 'These are the names of the sons of Israel who went to Egypt with
            Jacob, each with his family:'
        - verse: Reuben, Simeon, Levi and Judah;
        - verse: Issachar, Zebulun and Benjamin;
        - verse: Dan and Naphtali; Gad and Asher.
        - verse: The descendants of Jacob numbered seventy in all; Joseph was already
            in Egypt.
      - pageID: 22001
        verses:
        - verse: Now Joseph and all his brothers and all that generation died,
        - verse: but the Israelites were exceedingly fruitful; they multiplied greatly,
            increased in numbers and became so numerous that the land was filled
            with them.
        - verse: Then a new king, to whom Joseph meant nothing, came to power in
            Egypt.
        - verse: "\u201CLook,\u201D he said to his people, \u201Cthe Israelites have
            become far too numerous for us."
      - pageID: 32001
        verses:
        - verse: "Come, we must deal shrewdly with them or they will become even
            more numerous and, if war breaks out, will join our enemies, fight against
            us and leave the country.\u201D"
        - verse: So they put slave masters over them to oppress them with forced
            labor, and they built Pithom and Rameses as store cities for Pharaoh.
        - verse: But the more they were oppressed, the more they multiplied and spread;
            so the Egyptians came to dread the Israelites
        - verse: and worked them ruthlessly.
      - pageID: 42001
        verses:
        - verse: They made their lives bitter with harsh labor in brick and mortar
            and with all kinds of work in the fields; in all their harsh labor the
            Egyptians worked them ruthlessly.
        - verse: The king of Egypt said to the Hebrew midwives, whose names were
            Shiphrah and Puah,
        - verse: "\u201CWhen you are helping the Hebrew women during childbirth on
            the delivery stool, if you see that the baby is a boy, kill him; but
            if it is a girl, let her live.\u201D"
      - pageID: 52001
        verses:
        - verse: The midwives, however, feared God and did not do what the king of
            Egypt had told them to do; they let the boys live.
        - verse: "Then the king of Egypt summoned the midwives and asked them, \u201CWhy
            have you done this? Why have you let the boys live?\u201D"
        - verse: "The midwives answered Pharaoh, \u201CHebrew women are not like
            Egyptian women; they are vigorous and give birth before the midwives
            arrive.\u201D"
      - pageID: 62001
        verses:
        - verse: So God was kind to the midwives and the people increased and became
            even more numerous.
        - verse: And because the midwives feared God, he gave them families of their
            own.
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh gave this order to all his people: \u201CEvery Hebrew
            boy that is born you must throw into the Nile, but let every girl live.\u201D"
    - passageID: 2002
      title: The Birth of Moses
      pages:
      - pageID: 12002
        verses:
        - verse: Now a man of the tribe of Levi married a Levite woman,
        - verse: and she became pregnant and gave birth to a son. When she saw that
            he was a fine child, she hid him for three months.
        - verse: But when she could hide him no longer, she got a papyrus basket
            for him and coated it with tar and pitch. Then she placed the child in
            it and put it among the reeds along the bank of the Nile.
        - verse: His sister stood at a distance to see what would happen to him.
      - pageID: 22002
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh\u2019s daughter went down to the Nile to bathe, and
            her attendants were walking along the riverbank. She saw the basket among
            the reeds and sent her female slave to get it."
        - verse: "She opened it and saw the baby. He was crying, and she felt sorry
            for him. \u201CThis is one of the Hebrew babies,\u201D she said."
      - pageID: 32002
        verses:
        - verse: "Then his sister asked Pharaoh\u2019s daughter, \u201CShall I go
            and get one of the Hebrew women to nurse the baby for you?\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CYes, go,\u201D she answered. So the girl went and got the
            baby\u2019s mother."
        - verse: "Pharaoh\u2019s daughter said to her, \u201CTake this baby and nurse
            him for me, and I will pay you.\u201D So the woman took the baby and
            nursed him."
      - pageID: 42002
        verses:
        - verse: "When the child grew older, she took him to Pharaoh\u2019s daughter
            and he became her son. She named him Moses, saying, \u201CI drew him
            out of the water.\u201D"
    - passageID: 2003
      title: Moses Flees to Midian
      pages:
      - pageID: 42003
        verses:
        - verse: One day, after Moses had grown up, he went out to where his own
            people were and watched them at their hard labor. He saw an Egyptian
            beating a Hebrew, one of his own people.
        - verse: Looking this way and that and seeing no one, he killed the Egyptian
            and hid him in the sand.
      - pageID: 52003
        verses:
        - verse: "The next day he went out and saw two Hebrews fighting. He asked
            the one in the wrong, \u201CWhy are you hitting your fellow Hebrew?\u201D"
        - verse: "The man said, \u201CWho made you ruler and judge over us? Are you
            thinking of killing me as you killed the Egyptian?\u201D Then Moses was
            afraid and thought, \u201CWhat I did must have become known.\u201D"
      - pageID: 62003
        verses:
        - verse: When Pharaoh heard of this, he tried to kill Moses, but Moses fled
            from Pharaoh and went to live in Midian, where he sat down by a well.
        - verse: "Now a priest of Midian had seven daughters, and they came to draw
            water and fill the troughs to water their father\u2019s flock."
      - pageID: 72003
        verses:
        - verse: Some shepherds came along and drove them away, but Moses got up
            and came to their rescue and watered their flock.
      - pageID: 82003
        verses:
        - verse: "When the girls returned to Reuel their father, he asked them, \u201CWhy
            have you returned so early today?\u201D"
        - verse: "They answered, \u201CAn Egyptian rescued us from the shepherds.
            He even drew water for us and watered the flock.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CAnd where is he?\u201D Reuel asked his daughters. \u201CWhy
            did you leave him? Invite him to have something to eat.\u201D"
      - pageID: 92003
        verses:
        - verse: Moses agreed to stay with the man, who gave his daughter Zipporah
            to Moses in marriage.
        - verse: "Zipporah gave birth to a son, and Moses named him Gershom, saying,
            \u201CI have become a foreigner in a foreign land.\u201D"
      - pageID: 102003
        verses:
        - verse: During that long period, the king of Egypt died. The Israelites
            groaned in their slavery and cried out, and their cry for help because
            of their slavery went up to God.
        - verse: God heard their groaning and he remembered his covenant with Abraham,
            with Isaac and with Jacob.
        - verse: So God looked on the Israelites and was concerned about them.
    - passageID: 2004
      title: Moses and the Burning Bush
      pages:
      - pageID: 12004
        verses:
        - verse: Now Moses was tending the flock of Jethro his father-in-law, the
            priest of Midian, and he led the flock to the far side of the wilderness
            and came to Horeb, the mountain of God.
        - verse: There the angel of the Lord appeared to him in flames of fire from
            within a bush. Moses saw that though the bush was on fire it did not
            burn up.
      - pageID: 22004
        verses:
        - verse: "So Moses thought, \u201CI will go over and see this strange sight\u2014why
            the bush does not burn up.\u201D"
        - verse: "When the Lord saw that he had gone over to look, God called to
            him from within the bush, \u201CMoses! Moses!\u201D\r\n\r\nAnd Moses
            said, \u201CHere I am.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CDo not come any closer,\u201D God said. \u201CTake off your
            sandals, for the place where you are standing is holy ground.\u201D"
      - pageID: 32004
        verses:
        - verse: "Then he said, \u201CI am the God of your father, the God of Abraham,
            the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob.\u201D At this, Moses hid his face,
            because he was afraid to look at God."
        - verse: "The Lord said, \u201CI have indeed seen the misery of my people
            in Egypt. I have heard them crying out because of their slave drivers,
            and I am concerned about their suffering."
      - pageID: 42004
        verses:
        - verse: "So I have come down to rescue them from the hand of the Egyptians
            and to bring them up out of that land into a good and spacious land,
            a land flowing with milk and honey\u2014the home of the Canaanites, Hittites,
            Amorites, Perizzites, Hivites and Jebusites."
        - verse: And now the cry of the Israelites has reached me, and I have seen
            the way the Egyptians are oppressing them.
        - verse: "So now, go. I am sending you to Pharaoh to bring my people the
            Israelites out of Egypt.\u201D"
      - pageID: 52004
        verses:
        - verse: "But Moses said to God, \u201CWho am I that I should go to Pharaoh
            and bring the Israelites out of Egypt?\u201D"
        - verse: "and God said, \u201CI will be with you. And this will be the sign
            to you that it is I who have sent you: When you have brought the people
            out of Egypt, you will worship God on this mountain.\u201D"
      - pageID: 62004
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses said to God, \u201CSuppose I go to the Israelites and say
            to them, \u2018The God of your fathers has sent me to you,\u2019 and
            they ask me, \u2018What is his name?\u2019 Then what shall I tell them?\u201D"
        - verse: "God said to Moses, \u201CI am who I am. This is what you are to
            say to the Israelites: \u2018I am has sent me to you.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 72004
        verses:
        - verse: "God also said to Moses, \u201CSay to the Israelites, \u2018The
            Lord, the God of your fathers\u2014the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac
            and the God of Jacob\u2014has sent me to you.\u2019\r\n\r\n\u201CThis
            is my name forever,\r\n    the name you shall call me\r\n    from generation
            to generation."
        - verse: "\u201CGo, assemble the elders of Israel and say to them, \u2018The
            Lord, the God of your fathers\u2014the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob\u2014appeared
            to me and said: I have watched over you and have seen what has been done
            to you in Egypt."
      - pageID: 82004
        verses:
        - verse: "And I have promised to bring you up out of your misery in Egypt
            into the land of the Canaanites, Hittites, Amorites, Perizzites, Hivites
            and Jebusites\u2014a land flowing with milk and honey.\u2019"
        - verse: "\u201CThe elders of Israel will listen to you. Then you and the
            elders are to go to the king of Egypt and say to him, \u2018The Lord,
            the God of the Hebrews, has met with us. Let us take a three-day journey
            into the wilderness to offer sacrifices to the Lord our God.\u2019"
      - pageID: 92004
        verses:
        - verse: But I know that the king of Egypt will not let you go unless a mighty
            hand compels him.
        - verse: So I will stretch out my hand and strike the Egyptians with all
            the wonders that I will perform among them. After that, he will let you
            go.
        - verse: "\u201CAnd I will make the Egyptians favorably disposed toward this
            people, so that when you leave you will not go empty-handed."
      - pageID: 102004
        verses:
        - verse: "Every woman is to ask her neighbor and any woman living in her
            house for articles of silver and gold and for clothing, which you will
            put on your sons and daughters. And so you will plunder the Egyptians.\u201D"
    - passageID: 2005
      title: Signs for Moses
      pages:
      - pageID: 12005
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses answered, \u201CWhat if they do not believe me or listen
            to me and say, \u2018The Lord did not appear to you\u2019?\u201D"
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to him, \u201CWhat is that in your hand?\u201D\r\n\r\n\u201CA
            staff,\u201D he replied."
        - verse: "The Lord said, \u201CThrow it on the ground.\u201D\r\n\r\nMoses
            threw it on the ground and it became a snake, and he ran from it."
      - pageID: 22005
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to him, \u201CReach out your hand and take it
            by the tail.\u201D So Moses reached out and took hold of the snake and
            it turned back into a staff in his hand."
        - verse: "\u201CThis,\u201D said the Lord, \u201Cis so that they may believe
            that the Lord, the God of their fathers\u2014the God of Abraham, the
            God of Isaac and the God of Jacob\u2014has appeared to you.\u201D"
      - pageID: 32005
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said, \u201CPut your hand inside your cloak.\u201D
            So Moses put his hand into his cloak, and when he took it out, the skin
            was leprous\u2014it had become as white as snow."
        - verse: "\u201CNow put it back into your cloak,\u201D he said. So Moses
            put his hand back into his cloak, and when he took it out, it was restored,
            like the rest of his flesh."
        - verse: "Then the Lord said, \u201CIf they do not believe you or pay attention
            to the first sign, they may believe the second."
      - pageID: 42005
        verses:
        - verse: "But if they do not believe these two signs or listen to you, take
            some water from the Nile and pour it on the dry ground. The water you
            take from the river will become blood on the ground.\u201D"
        - verse: "Moses said to the Lord, \u201CPardon your servant, Lord. I have
            never been eloquent, neither in the past nor since you have spoken to
            your servant. I am slow of speech and tongue.\u201D"
      - pageID: 52005
        verses:
        - verse: "The Lord said to him, \u201CWho gave human beings their mouths?
            Who makes them deaf or mute? Who gives them sight or makes them blind?
            Is it not I, the Lord?"
        - verse: "Now go; I will help you speak and will teach you what to say.\u201D"
        - verse: "But Moses said, \u201CPardon your servant, Lord. Please send someone
            else.\u201D"
      - pageID: 62005
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord\u2019s anger burned against Moses and he said, \u201CWhat
            about your brother, Aaron the Levite? I know he can speak well. He is
            already on his way to meet you, and he will be glad to see you."
        - verse: You shall speak to him and put words in his mouth; I will help both
            of you speak and will teach you what to do.
        - verse: He will speak to the people for you, and it will be as if he were
            your mouth and as if you were God to him.
        - verse: "But take this staff in your hand so you can perform the signs with
            it.\u201D"
    - passageID: 2006
      title: Moses Returns to Egypt
      pages:
      - pageID: 12006
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Moses went back to Jethro his father-in-law and said to him,
            \u201CLet me return to my own people in Egypt to see if any of them are
            still alive.\u201D\r\n\r\nJethro said, \u201CGo, and I wish you well.\u201D"
        - verse: "Now the Lord had said to Moses in Midian, \u201CGo back to Egypt,
            for all those who wanted to kill you are dead.\u201D"
      - pageID: 22006
        verses:
        - verse: So Moses took his wife and sons, put them on a donkey and started
            back to Egypt. And he took the staff of God in his hand.
        - verse: "The Lord said to Moses, \u201CWhen you return to Egypt, see that
            you perform before Pharaoh all the wonders I have given you the power
            to do. But I will harden his heart so that he will not let the people
            go."
        - verse: "Then say to Pharaoh, \u2018This is what the Lord says: Israel is
            my firstborn son,"
      - pageID: 32006
        verses:
        - verse: "and I told you, \u201CLet my son go, so he may worship me.\u201D
            But you refused to let him go; so I will kill your firstborn son.\u2019\u201D"
        - verse: At a lodging place on the way, the Lord met Moses and was about
            to kill him.
      - pageID: 42006
        verses:
        - verse: "But Zipporah took a flint knife, cut off her son\u2019s foreskin
            and touched Moses\u2019 feet with it. \u201CSurely you are a bridegroom
            of blood to me,\u201D she said."
        - verse: "So the Lord let him alone. (At that time she said \u201Cbridegroom
            of blood,\u201D referring to circumcision.)"
      - pageID: 52006
        verses:
        - verse: "The Lord said to Aaron, \u201CGo into the wilderness to meet Moses.\u201D
            So he met Moses at the mountain of God and kissed him."
        - verse: Then Moses told Aaron everything the Lord had sent him to say, and
            also about all the signs he had commanded him to perform.
        - verse: Moses and Aaron brought together all the elders of the Israelites,
      - pageID: 62006
        verses:
        - verse: and Aaron told them everything the Lord had said to Moses. He also
            performed the signs before the people,
        - verse: and they believed. And when they heard that the Lord was concerned
            about them and had seen their misery, they bowed down and worshiped.
    - passageID: 2007
      title: Bricks Without Straw
      pages:
      - pageID: 12007
        verses:
        - verse: "Afterward Moses and Aaron went to Pharaoh and said, \u201CThis
            is what the Lord, the God of Israel, says: \u2018Let my people go, so
            that they may hold a festival to me in the wilderness.\u2019\u201D"
        - verse: "Pharaoh said, \u201CWho is the Lord, that I should obey him and
            let Israel go? I do not know the Lord and I will not let Israel go.\u201D"
      - pageID: 22007
        verses:
        - verse: "Then they said, \u201CThe God of the Hebrews has met with us. Now
            let us take a three-day journey into the wilderness to offer sacrifices
            to the Lord our God, or he may strike us with plagues or with the sword.\u201D"
        - verse: "But the king of Egypt said, \u201CMoses and Aaron, why are you
            taking the people away from their labor? Get back to your work!\u201D"
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh said, \u201CLook, the people of the land are now numerous,
            and you are stopping them from working.\u201D"
      - pageID: 32007
        verses:
        - verse: 'That same day Pharaoh gave this order to the slave drivers and
            overseers in charge of the people:'
        - verse: "\u201CYou are no longer to supply the people with straw for making
            bricks; let them go and gather their own straw."
        - verse: "But require them to make the same number of bricks as before; don\u2019t
            reduce the quota. They are lazy; that is why they are crying out, \u2018Let
            us go and sacrifice to our God.\u2019"
      - pageID: 42007
        verses:
        - verse: "Make the work harder for the people so that they keep working and
            pay no attention to lies.\u201D"
        - verse: "Then the slave drivers and the overseers went out and said to the
            people, \u201CThis is what Pharaoh says: \u2018I will not give you any
            more straw."
        - verse: "Go and get your own straw wherever you can find it, but your work
            will not be reduced at all.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 52007
        verses:
        - verse: "So the people scattered all over Egypt to gather stubble to use
            for straw. 13 The slave drivers kept pressing them, saying, \u201CComplete
            the work required of you for each day, just as when you had straw.\u201D"
        - verse: "The slave drivers kept pressing them, saying, \u201CComplete the
            work required of you for each day, just as when you had straw.\u201D"
        - verse: "And Pharaoh\u2019s slave drivers beat the Israelite overseers they
            had appointed, demanding, \u201CWhy haven\u2019t you met your quota of
            bricks yesterday or today, as before?\u201D"
      - pageID: 62007
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Israelite overseers went and appealed to Pharaoh: \u201CWhy
            have you treated your servants this way?"
        - verse: "Your servants are given no straw, yet we are told, \u2018Make bricks!\u2019
            Your servants are being beaten, but the fault is with your own people.\u201D"
      - pageID: 72007
        verses:
        - verse: "Pharaoh said, \u201CLazy, that\u2019s what you are\u2014lazy! That
            is why you keep saying, \u2018Let us go and sacrifice to the Lord.\u2019"
        - verse: "Now get to work. You will not be given any straw, yet you must
            produce your full quota of bricks.\u201D"
      - pageID: 82007
        verses:
        - verse: "The Israelite overseers realized they were in trouble when they
            were told, \u201CYou are not to reduce the number of bricks required
            of you for each day.\u201D"
        - verse: When they left Pharaoh, they found Moses and Aaron waiting to meet
            them,
        - verse: "and they said, \u201CMay the Lord look on you and judge you! You
            have made us obnoxious to Pharaoh and his officials and have put a sword
            in their hand to kill us.\u201D"
    - passageID: 2008
      title: God Promises Deliverence
      pages:
      - pageID: 12008
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses returned to the Lord and said, \u201CWhy, Lord, why have
            you brought trouble on this people? Is this why you sent me?"
        - verse: "Ever since I went to Pharaoh to speak in your name, he has brought
            trouble on this people, and you have not rescued your people at all.\u201D"
      - pageID: 22008
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CNow you will see what I will
            do to Pharaoh: Because of my mighty hand he will let them go; because
            of my mighty hand he will drive them out of his country.\u201D"
        - verse: "God also said to Moses, \u201CI am the Lord."
      - pageID: 32008
        verses:
        - verse: 'I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac and to Jacob as God Almighty, but
            by my name the Lord I did not make myself fully known to them. '
        - verse: 'I also established my covenant with them to give them the land
            of Canaan, where they resided as foreigners. '
        - verse: Moreover, I have heard the groaning of the Israelites, whom the
            Egyptians are enslaving, and I have remembered my covenant.
      - pageID: 42008
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CTherefore, say to the Israelites: \u2018I am the Lord, and
            I will bring you out from under the yoke of the Egyptians. I will free
            you from being slaves to them, and I will redeem you with an outstretched
            arm and with mighty acts of judgment."
      - pageID: 52008
        verses:
        - verse: I will take you as my own people, and I will be your God. Then you
            will know that I am the Lord your God, who brought you out from under
            the yoke of the Egyptians.
        - verse: "And I will bring you to the land I swore with uplifted hand to
            give to Abraham, to Isaac and to Jacob. I will give it to you as a possession.
            I am the Lord.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 62008
        verses:
        - verse: Moses reported this to the Israelites, but they did not listen to
            him because of their discouragement and harsh labor.
        - verse: Then the Lord said to Moses,
        - verse: "\u201CGo, tell Pharaoh king of Egypt to let the Israelites go out
            of his country.\u201D"
      - pageID: 72008
        verses:
        - verse: "But Moses said to the Lord, \u201CIf the Israelites will not listen
            to me, why would Pharaoh listen to me, since I speak with faltering lips?\u201D"
    - passageID: 2009
      title: Family Record of Moses and Aaron
      pages:
      - pageID: 12009
        verses:
        - verse: Now the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron about the Israelites and Pharaoh
            king of Egypt, and he commanded them to bring the Israelites out of Egypt.
        - verse: 'These were the heads of their families:



            The sons
            of Reuben the firstborn son of Israel were Hanok and Pallu, Hezron and
            Karmi. These were the clans of Reuben.'
      - pageID: 22009
        verses:
        - verse: The sons of Simeon were Jemuel, Jamin, Ohad, Jakin, Zohar and Shaul
            the son of a Canaanite woman. These were the clans of Simeon.
        - verse: 'These were the names of the sons of Levi according to their records:
            Gershon, Kohath and Merari. Levi lived 137 years.'
      - pageID: 32009
        verses:
        - verse: The sons of Gershon, by clans, were Libni and Shimei.
        - verse: The sons of Kohath were Amram, Izhar, Hebron and Uzziel. Kohath
            lived 133 years.
        - verse: 'The sons of Merari were Mahli and Mushi.



            These were
            the clans of Levi according to their records.'
      - pageID: 42009
        verses:
        - verse: "Amram married his father\u2019s sister Jochebed, who bore him Aaron
            and Moses. Amram lived 137 years."
        - verse: The sons of Izhar were Korah, Nepheg and Zikri.
        - verse: The sons of Uzziel were Mishael, Elzaphan and Sithri.
      - pageID: 52009
        verses:
        - verse: Aaron married Elisheba, daughter of Amminadab and sister of Nahshon,
            and she bore him Nadab and Abihu, Eleazar and Ithamar.
        - verse: The sons of Korah were Assir, Elkanah and Abiasaph. These were the
            Korahite clans.
      - pageID: 62009
        verses:
        - verse: 'Eleazar son of Aaron married one of the daughters of Putiel, and
            she bore him Phinehas.



            These were the heads of the Levite
            families, clan by clan.'
        - verse: "It was this Aaron and Moses to whom the Lord said, \u201CBring
            the Israelites out of Egypt by their divisions.\u201D"
        - verse: "They were the ones who spoke to Pharaoh king of Egypt about bringing
            the Israelites out of Egypt\u2014this same Moses and Aaron."
    - passageID: 2010
      title: Aaron to Speak for Moses
      pages:
      - pageID: 12010
        verses:
        - verse: Now when the Lord spoke to Moses in Egypt,
        - verse: "he said to him, \u201CI am the Lord. Tell Pharaoh king of Egypt
            everything I tell you.\u201D"
        - verse: "But Moses said to the Lord, \u201CSince I speak with faltering
            lips, why would Pharaoh listen to me?\u201D"
      - pageID: 22010
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CSee, I have made you like God
            to Pharaoh, and your brother Aaron will be your prophet."
        - verse: You are to say everything I command you, and your brother Aaron
            is to tell Pharaoh to let the Israelites go out of his country.
      - pageID: 32010
        verses:
        - verse: "But I will harden Pharaoh\u2019s heart, and though I multiply my
            signs and wonders in Egypt,"
        - verse: he will not listen to you. Then I will lay my hand on Egypt and
            with mighty acts of judgment I will bring out my divisions, my people
            the Israelites.
        - verse: "And the Egyptians will know that I am the Lord when I stretch out
            my hand against Egypt and bring the Israelites out of it.\u201D"
      - pageID: 42010
        verses:
        - verse: Moses and Aaron did just as the Lord commanded them.
        - verse: Moses was eighty years old and Aaron eighty-three when they spoke
            to Pharaoh.
    - passageID: 2011
      title: Aaron 's Staff Becomes a Snake
      pages:
      - pageID: 12011
        verses:
        - verse: 'The Lord said to Moses and Aaron, '
        - verse: "\u201CWhen Pharaoh says to you, \u2018Perform a miracle,\u2019
            then say to Aaron, \u2018Take your staff and throw it down before Pharaoh,\u2019
            and it will become a snake.\u201D"
        - verse: So Moses and Aaron went to Pharaoh and did just as the Lord commanded.
            Aaron threw his staff down in front of Pharaoh and his officials, and
            it became a snake.
      - pageID: 22011
        verses:
        - verse: 'Pharaoh then summoned wise men and sorcerers, and the Egyptian
            magicians also did the same things by their secret arts:'
        - verse: "Each one threw down his staff and it became a snake. But Aaron\u2019s
            staff swallowed up their staffs."
        - verse: "Yet Pharaoh\u2019s heart became hard and he would not listen to
            them, just as the Lord had said."
    - passageID: 2012
      title: The Plague of Blood
      pages:
      - pageID: 22012
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CPharaoh\u2019s heart is unyielding;
            he refuses to let the people go."
      - pageID: 32012
        verses:
        - verse: Go to Pharaoh in the morning as he goes out to the river. Confront
            him on the bank of the Nile, and take in your hand the staff that was
            changed into a snake.
        - verse: "Then say to him, \u2018The Lord, the God of the Hebrews, has sent
            me to say to you: Let my people go, so that they may worship me in the
            wilderness. But until now you have not listened."
        - verse: 'This is what the Lord says: By this you will know that I am the
            Lord: With the staff that is in my hand I will strike the water of the
            Nile, and it will be changed into blood.'
      - pageID: 42012
        verses:
        - verse: "The fish in the Nile will die, and the river will stink; the Egyptians
            will not be able to drink its water.\u2019\u201D"
        - verse: "The Lord said to Moses, \u201CTell Aaron, \u2018Take your staff
            and stretch out your hand over the waters of Egypt\u2014over the streams
            and canals, over the ponds and all the reservoirs\u2014and they will
            turn to blood.\u2019 Blood will be everywhere in Egypt, even in vessels
            of wood and stone.\u201D"
      - pageID: 52012
        verses:
        - verse: Moses and Aaron did just as the Lord had commanded. He raised his
            staff in the presence of Pharaoh and his officials and struck the water
            of the Nile, and all the water was changed into blood.
        - verse: The fish in the Nile died, and the river smelled so bad that the
            Egyptians could not drink its water. Blood was everywhere in Egypt.
      - pageID: 62012
        verses:
        - verse: "But the Egyptian magicians did the same things by their secret
            arts, and Pharaoh\u2019s heart became hard; he would not listen to Moses
            and Aaron, just as the Lord had said."
        - verse: Instead, he turned and went into his palace, and did not take even
            this to heart.
        - verse: And all the Egyptians dug along the Nile to get drinking water,
            because they could not drink the water of the river.
    - passageID: 2013
      title: The Plague of Frogs
      pages:
      - pageID: 12013
        verses:
        - verse: Seven days passed after the Lord struck the Nile.
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CGo to Pharaoh and say to him,
            \u2018This is what the Lord says: Let my people go, so that they may
            worship me."
        - verse: If you refuse to let them go, I will send a plague of frogs on your
            whole country.
      - pageID: 22013
        verses:
        - verse: The Nile will teem with frogs. They will come up into your palace
            and your bedroom and onto your bed, into the houses of your officials
            and on your people, and into your ovens and kneading troughs.
        - verse: "The frogs will come up on you and your people and all your officials.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 32013
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CTell Aaron, \u2018Stretch out
            your hand with your staff over the streams and canals and ponds, and
            make frogs come up on the land of Egypt.\u2019\u201D"
        - verse: So Aaron stretched out his hand over the waters of Egypt, and the
            frogs came up and covered the land.
      - pageID: 42013
        verses:
        - verse: But the magicians did the same things by their secret arts; they
            also made frogs come up on the land of Egypt.
        - verse: "Pharaoh summoned Moses and Aaron and said, \u201CPray to the Lord
            to take the frogs away from me and my people, and I will let your people
            go to offer sacrifices to the Lord.\u201D"
      - pageID: 52013
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses said to Pharaoh, \u201CI leave to you the honor of setting
            the time for me to pray for you and your officials and your people that
            you and your houses may be rid of the frogs, except for those that remain
            in the Nile.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CTomorrow,\u201D Pharaoh said.\r\n\r\nMoses replied, \u201CIt
            will be as you say, so that you may know there is no one like the Lord
            our God."
        - verse: "The frogs will leave you and your houses, your officials and your
            people; they will remain only in the Nile.\u201D"
      - pageID: 62013
        verses:
        - verse: After Moses and Aaron left Pharaoh, Moses cried out to the Lord
            about the frogs he had brought on Pharaoh.
        - verse: And the Lord did what Moses asked. The frogs died in the houses,
            in the courtyards and in the fields.
        - verse: They were piled into heaps, and the land reeked of them.
        - verse: But when Pharaoh saw that there was relief, he hardened his heart
            and would not listen to Moses and Aaron, just as the Lord had said.
    - passageID: 2014
      title: The Plague of Gnats
      pages:
      - pageID: 12014
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CTell Aaron, \u2018Stretch out
            your staff and strike the dust of the ground,\u2019 and throughout the
            land of Egypt the dust will become gnats.\u201D"
        - verse: They did this, and when Aaron stretched out his hand with the staff
            and struck the dust of the ground, gnats came on people and animals.
            All the dust throughout the land of Egypt became gnats.
      - pageID: 22014
        verses:
        - verse: 'But when the magicians tried to produce gnats by their secret arts,
            they could not.



            Since the gnats were on people and animals
            everywhere,'
        - verse: "the magicians said to Pharaoh, \u201CThis is the finger of God.\u201D
            But Pharaoh\u2019s heart was hard and he would not listen, just as the
            Lord had said."
    - passageID: 2015
      title: The Plague of Flies
      pages:
      - pageID: 12015
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CGet up early in the morning
            and confront Pharaoh as he goes to the river and say to him, \u2018This
            is what the Lord says: Let my people go, so that they may worship me."
        - verse: If you do not let my people go, I will send swarms of flies on you
            and your officials, on your people and into your houses. The houses of
            the Egyptians will be full of flies; even the ground will be covered
            with them.
      - pageID: 22015
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018But on that day I will deal differently with the land
            of Goshen, where my people live; no swarms of flies will be there, so
            that you will know that I, the Lord, am in this land."
        - verse: "I will make a distinction between my people and your people. This
            sign will occur tomorrow.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 32015
        verses:
        - verse: "And the Lord did this. Dense swarms of flies poured into Pharaoh\u2019s
            palace and into the houses of his officials; throughout Egypt the land
            was ruined by the flies."
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh summoned Moses and Aaron and said, \u201CGo, sacrifice
            to your God here in the land.\u201D"
      - pageID: 42015
        verses:
        - verse: "But Moses said, \u201CThat would not be right. The sacrifices we
            offer the Lord our God would be detestable to the Egyptians. And if we
            offer sacrifices that are detestable in their eyes, will they not stone
            us?"
        - verse: "We must take a three-day journey into the wilderness to offer sacrifices
            to the Lord our God, as he commands us.\u201D"
      - pageID: 52015
        verses:
        - verse: "Pharaoh said, \u201CI will let you go to offer sacrifices to the
            Lord your God in the wilderness, but you must not go very far. Now pray
            for me.\u201D"
        - verse: "Moses answered, \u201CAs soon as I leave you, I will pray to the
            Lord, and tomorrow the flies will leave Pharaoh and his officials and
            his people. Only let Pharaoh be sure that he does not act deceitfully
            again by not letting the people go to offer sacrifices to the Lord.\u201D"
        - verse: Then Moses left Pharaoh and prayed to the Lord,
      - pageID: 62015
        verses:
        - verse: and the Lord did what Moses asked. The flies left Pharaoh and his
            officials and his people; not a fly remained.
        - verse: But this time also Pharaoh hardened his heart and would not let
            the people go.
    - passageID: 2016
      title: The Plague of Livestock
      pages:
      - pageID: 12016
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CGo to Pharaoh and say to him,
            \u2018This is what the Lord, the God of the Hebrews, says: \u201CLet
            my people go, so that they may worship me.\u201D"
        - verse: If you refuse to let them go and continue to hold them back,
        - verse: "the hand of the Lord will bring a terrible plague on your livestock
            in the field\u2014on your horses, donkeys and camels and on your cattle,
            sheep and goats."
      - pageID: 22016
        verses:
        - verse: "But the Lord will make a distinction between the livestock of Israel
            and that of Egypt, so that no animal belonging to the Israelites will
            die.\u2019\u201D"
        - verse: "The Lord set a time and said, \u201CTomorrow the Lord will do this
            in the land.\u201D"
      - pageID: 32016
        verses:
        - verse: 'And the next day the Lord did it: All the livestock of the Egyptians
            died, but not one animal belonging to the Israelites died.'
        - verse: Pharaoh investigated and found that not even one of the animals
            of the Israelites had died. Yet his heart was unyielding and he would
            not let the people go.
    - passageID: 2017
      title: The Plague of Boils
      pages:
      - pageID: 12017
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses and Aaron, \u201CTake handfuls of soot
            from a furnace and have Moses toss it into the air in the presence of
            Pharaoh."
        - verse: "It will become fine dust over the whole land of Egypt, and festering
            boils will break out on people and animals throughout the land.\u201D"
      - pageID: 22017
        verses:
        - verse: So they took soot from a furnace and stood before Pharaoh. Moses
            tossed it into the air, and festering boils broke out on people and animals.
        - verse: The magicians could not stand before Moses because of the boils
            that were on them and on all the Egyptians.
        - verse: "But the Lord hardened Pharaoh\u2019s heart and he would not listen
            to Moses and Aaron, just as the Lord had said to Moses."
    - passageID: 2018
      title: The Plague of Hail
      pages:
      - pageID: 12018
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CGet up early in the morning,
            confront Pharaoh and say to him, \u2018This is what the Lord, the God
            of the Hebrews, says: Let my people go, so that they may worship me,"
        - verse: or this time I will send the full force of my plagues against you
            and against your officials and your people, so you may know that there
            is no one like me in all the earth.
      - pageID: 22018
        verses:
        - verse: For by now I could have stretched out my hand and struck you and
            your people with a plague that would have wiped you off the earth.
        - verse: But I have raised you up for this very purpose, that I might show
            you my power and that my name might be proclaimed in all the earth.
        - verse: You still set yourself against my people and will not let them go.
      - pageID: 32018
        verses:
        - verse: Therefore, at this time tomorrow I will send the worst hailstorm
            that has ever fallen on Egypt, from the day it was founded till now.
        - verse: "Give an order now to bring your livestock and everything you have
            in the field to a place of shelter, because the hail will fall on every
            person and animal that has not been brought in and is still out in the
            field, and they will die.\u2019\u201D"
      - pageID: 42018
        verses:
        - verse: Those officials of Pharaoh who feared the word of the Lord hurried
            to bring their slaves and their livestock inside.
        - verse: But those who ignored the word of the Lord left their slaves and
            livestock in the field.
      - pageID: 52018
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CStretch out your hand toward
            the sky so that hail will fall all over Egypt\u2014on people and animals
            and on everything growing in the fields of Egypt.\u201D"
        - verse: When Moses stretched out his staff toward the sky, the Lord sent
            thunder and hail, and lightning flashed down to the ground. So the Lord
            rained hail on the land of Egypt;
      - pageID: 62018
        verses:
        - verse: hail fell and lightning flashed back and forth. It was the worst
            storm in all the land of Egypt since it had become a nation.
        - verse: "Throughout Egypt hail struck everything in the fields\u2014both
            people and animals; it beat down everything growing in the fields and
            stripped every tree."
        - verse: The only place it did not hail was the land of Goshen, where the
            Israelites were.
      - pageID: 72018
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh summoned Moses and Aaron. \u201CThis time I have sinned,\u201D
            he said to them. \u201CThe Lord is in the right, and I and my people
            are in the wrong."
        - verse: "Pray to the Lord, for we have had enough thunder and hail. I will
            let you go; you don\u2019t have to stay any longer.\u201D"
        - verse: "Moses replied, \u201CWhen I have gone out of the city, I will spread
            out my hands in prayer to the Lord. The thunder will stop and there will
            be no more hail, so you may know that the earth is the Lord\u2019s."
        - verse: "But I know that you and your officials still do not fear the Lord
            God.\u201D"
      - pageID: 82018
        verses:
        - verse: (The flax and barley were destroyed, since the barley had headed
            and the flax was in bloom.
        - verse: The wheat and spelt, however, were not destroyed, because they ripen
            later.)
        - verse: Then Moses left Pharaoh and went out of the city. He spread out
            his hands toward the Lord; the thunder and hail stopped, and the rain
            no longer poured down on the land.
      - pageID: 92018
        verses:
        - verse: 'When Pharaoh saw that the rain and hail and thunder had stopped,
            he sinned again: He and his officials hardened their hearts.'
        - verse: "So Pharaoh\u2019s heart was hard and he would not let the Israelites
            go, just as the Lord had said through Moses."
    - passageID: 2019
      title: The Plague of Locusts
      pages:
      - pageID: 12019
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CGo to Pharaoh, for I have hardened
            his heart and the hearts of his officials so that I may perform these
            signs of mine among them"
        - verse: "that you may tell your children and grandchildren how I dealt harshly
            with the Egyptians and how I performed my signs among them, and that
            you may know that I am the Lord.\u201D"
      - pageID: 22019
        verses:
        - verse: "So Moses and Aaron went to Pharaoh and said to him, \u201CThis
            is what the Lord, the God of the Hebrews, says: \u2018How long will you
            refuse to humble yourself before me? Let my people go, so that they may
            worship me."
        - verse: If you refuse to let them go, I will bring locusts into your country
            tomorrow.
      - pageID: 32019
        verses:
        - verse: They will cover the face of the ground so that it cannot be seen.
            They will devour what little you have left after the hail, including
            every tree that is growing in your fields.
        - verse: "They will fill your houses and those of all your officials and
            all the Egyptians\u2014something neither your parents nor your ancestors
            have ever seen from the day they settled in this land till now.\u2019\u201D
            Then Moses turned and left Pharaoh."
      - pageID: 42019
        verses:
        - verse: "Pharaoh\u2019s officials said to him, \u201CHow long will this
            man be a snare to us? Let the people go, so that they may worship the
            Lord their God. Do you not yet realize that Egypt is ruined?\u201D"
        - verse: "Then Moses and Aaron were brought back to Pharaoh. \u201CGo, worship
            the Lord your God,\u201D he said. \u201CBut tell me who will be going.\u201D"
      - pageID: 52019
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses answered, \u201CWe will go with our young and our old, with
            our sons and our daughters, and with our flocks and herds, because we
            are to celebrate a festival to the Lord.\u201D"
        - verse: "Pharaoh said, \u201CThe Lord be with you\u2014if I let you go,
            along with your women and children! Clearly you are bent on evil."
        - verse: "No! Have only the men go and worship the Lord, since that\u2019s
            what you have been asking for.\u201D Then Moses and Aaron were driven
            out of Pharaoh\u2019s presence."
      - pageID: 62019
        verses:
        - verse: "And the Lord said to Moses, \u201CStretch out your hand over Egypt
            so that locusts swarm over the land and devour everything growing in
            the fields, everything left by the hail.\u201D"
        - verse: So Moses stretched out his staff over Egypt, and the Lord made an
            east wind blow across the land all that day and all that night. By morning
            the wind had brought the locusts;
      - pageID: 72019
        verses:
        - verse: they invaded all Egypt and settled down in every area of the country
            in great numbers. Never before had there been such a plague of locusts,
            nor will there ever be again.
        - verse: "They covered all the ground until it was black. They devoured all
            that was left after the hail\u2014everything growing in the fields and
            the fruit on the trees. Nothing green remained on tree or plant in all
            the land of Egypt."
      - pageID: 82019
        verses:
        - verse: "Pharaoh quickly summoned Moses and Aaron and said, \u201CI have
            sinned against the Lord your God and against you."
        - verse: "Now forgive my sin once more and pray to the Lord your God to take
            this deadly plague away from me.\u201D"
        - verse: Moses then left Pharaoh and prayed to the Lord.
      - pageID: 92019
        verses:
        - verse: And the Lord changed the wind to a very strong west wind, which
            caught up the locusts and carried them into the Red Sea. Not a locust
            was left anywhere in Egypt.
        - verse: "But the Lord hardened Pharaoh\u2019s heart, and he would not let
            the Israelites go."
    - passageID: 2020
      title: The Plague of Darkness
      pages:
      - pageID: 12020
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CStretch out your hand toward
            the sky so that darkness spreads over Egypt\u2014darkness that can be
            felt.\u201D"
        - verse: So Moses stretched out his hand toward the sky, and total darkness
            covered all Egypt for three days.
        - verse: No one could see anyone else or move about for three days. Yet all
            the Israelites had light in the places where they lived.
      - pageID: 22020
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Pharaoh summoned Moses and said, \u201CGo, worship the Lord.
            Even your women and children may go with you; only leave your flocks
            and herds behind.\u201D"
        - verse: "But Moses said, \u201CYou must allow us to have sacrifices and
            burnt offerings to present to the Lord our God."
      - pageID: 32020
        verses:
        - verse: "Our livestock too must go with us; not a hoof is to be left behind.
            We have to use some of them in worshiping the Lord our God, and until
            we get there we will not know what we are to use to worship the Lord.\u201D"
        - verse: "But the Lord hardened Pharaoh\u2019s heart, and he was not willing
            to let them go."
      - pageID: 42020
        verses:
        - verse: "Pharaoh said to Moses, \u201CGet out of my sight! Make sure you
            do not appear before me again! The day you see my face you will die.\u201D"
        - verse: "\u201CJust as you say,\u201D Moses replied. \u201CI will never
            appear before you again.\u201D"
    - passageID: 2021
      title: The Plague on the Firstborn
      pages:
      - pageID: 12021
        verses:
        - verse: "Now the Lord had said to Moses, \u201CI will bring one more plague
            on Pharaoh and on Egypt. After that, he will let you go from here, and
            when he does, he will drive you out completely."
        - verse: "Tell the people that men and women alike are to ask their neighbors
            for articles of silver and gold.\u201D"
        - verse: "(The Lord made the Egyptians favorably disposed toward the people,
            and Moses himself was highly regarded in Egypt by Pharaoh\u2019s officials
            and by the people.)"
      - pageID: 22021
        verses:
        - verse: "So Moses said, \u201CThis is what the Lord says: \u2018About midnight
            I will go throughout Egypt."
        - verse: Every firstborn son in Egypt will die, from the firstborn son of
            Pharaoh, who sits on the throne, to the firstborn son of the female slave,
            who is at her hand mill, and all the firstborn of the cattle as well.
        - verse: "There will be loud wailing throughout Egypt\u2014worse than there
            has ever been or ever will be again."
      - pageID: 32021
        verses:
        - verse: "But among the Israelites not a dog will bark at any person or animal.\u2019
            Then you will know that the Lord makes a distinction between Egypt and
            Israel."
        - verse: "All these officials of yours will come to me, bowing down before
            me and saying, \u2018Go, you and all the people who follow you!\u2019
            After that I will leave.\u201D Then Moses, hot with anger, left Pharaoh."
      - pageID: 42021
        verses:
        - verse: "The Lord had said to Moses, \u201CPharaoh will refuse to listen
            to you\u2014so that my wonders may be multiplied in Egypt.\u201D"
        - verse: "Moses and Aaron performed all these wonders before Pharaoh, but
            the Lord hardened Pharaoh\u2019s heart, and he would not let the Israelites
            go out of his country."
    - passageID: 2022
      title: The Passover and the Festival of Uneleavened Bread
      pages:
      - pageID: 12022
        verses:
        - verse: The Lord said to Moses and Aaron in Egypt,
        - verse: "\u201CThis month is to be for you the first month, the first month
            of your year."
        - verse: Tell the whole community of Israel that on the tenth day of this
            month each man is to take a lamb for his family, one for each household.
      - pageID: 22022
        verses:
        - verse: If any household is too small for a whole lamb, they must share
            one with their nearest neighbor, having taken into account the number
            of people there are. You are to determine the amount of lamb needed in
            accordance with what each person will eat.
        - verse: The animals you choose must be year-old males without defect, and
            you may take them from the sheep or the goats.
      - pageID: 32022
        verses:
        - verse: Take care of them until the fourteenth day of the month, when all
            the members of the community of Israel must slaughter them at twilight.
        - verse: Then they are to take some of the blood and put it on the sides
            and tops of the doorframes of the houses where they eat the lambs.
      - pageID: 42022
        verses:
        - verse: That same night they are to eat the meat roasted over the fire,
            along with bitter herbs, and bread made without yeast.
        - verse: "Do not eat the meat raw or boiled in water, but roast it over a
            fire\u2014with the head, legs and internal organs."
        - verse: Do not leave any of it till morning; if some is left till morning,
            you must burn it.
      - pageID: 52022
        verses:
        - verse: "This is how you are to eat it: with your cloak tucked into your
            belt, your sandals on your feet and your staff in your hand. Eat it in
            haste; it is the Lord\u2019s Passover."
        - verse: "\u201COn that same night I will pass through Egypt and strike down
            every firstborn of both people and animals, and I will bring judgment
            on all the gods of Egypt. I am the Lord."
      - pageID: 62022
        verses:
        - verse: The blood will be a sign for you on the houses where you are, and
            when I see the blood, I will pass over you. No destructive plague will
            touch you when I strike Egypt.
        - verse: "\u201CThis is a day you are to commemorate; for the generations
            to come you shall celebrate it as a festival to the Lord\u2014a lasting
            ordinance."
      - pageID: 72022
        verses:
        - verse: For seven days you are to eat bread made without yeast. On the first
            day remove the yeast from your houses, for whoever eats anything with
            yeast in it from the first day through the seventh must be cut off from
            Israel.
        - verse: On the first day hold a sacred assembly, and another one on the
            seventh day. Do no work at all on these days, except to prepare food
            for everyone to eat; that is all you may do.
      - pageID: 82022
        verses:
        - verse: " \u201CCelebrate the Festival of Unleavened Bread, because it was
            on this very day that I brought your divisions out of Egypt. Celebrate
            this day as a lasting ordinance for the generations to come."
      - pageID: 92022
        verses:
        - verse: In the first month you are to eat bread made without yeast, from
            the evening of the fourteenth day until the evening of the twenty-first
            day.
        - verse: For seven days no yeast is to be found in your houses. And anyone,
            whether foreigner or native-born, who eats anything with yeast in it
            must be cut off from the community of Israel.
        - verse: "Eat nothing made with yeast. Wherever you live, you must eat unleavened
            bread.\u201D"
      - pageID: 102022
        verses:
        - verse: "Then Moses summoned all the elders of Israel and said to them,
            \u201CGo at once and select the animals for your families and slaughter
            the Passover lamb."
        - verse: Take a bunch of hyssop, dip it into the blood in the basin and put
            some of the blood on the top and on both sides of the doorframe. None
            of you shall go out of the door of your house until morning.
      - pageID: 112022
        verses:
        - verse: When the Lord goes through the land to strike down the Egyptians,
            he will see the blood on the top and sides of the doorframe and will
            pass over that doorway, and he will not permit the destroyer to enter
            your houses and strike you down.
      - pageID: 122022
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CObey these instructions as a lasting ordinance for you and
            your descendants."
        - verse: When you enter the land that the Lord will give you as he promised,
            observe this ceremony.
        - verse: "And when your children ask you, \u2018What does this ceremony mean
            to you?\u2019"
      - pageID: 132022
        verses:
        - verse: "then tell them, \u2018It is the Passover sacrifice to the Lord,
            who passed over the houses of the Israelites in Egypt and spared our
            homes when he struck down the Egyptians.\u2019\u201D Then the people
            bowed down and worshiped."
        - verse: The Israelites did just what the Lord commanded Moses and Aaron.
      - pageID: 142022
        verses:
        - verse: At midnight the Lord struck down all the firstborn in Egypt, from
            the firstborn of Pharaoh, who sat on the throne, to the firstborn of
            the prisoner, who was in the dungeon, and the firstborn of all the livestock
            as well.
        - verse: Pharaoh and all his officials and all the Egyptians got up during
            the night, and there was loud wailing in Egypt, for there was not a house
            without someone dead.
    - passageID: 2023
      title: The Exodus
      pages:
      - pageID: 12023
        verses:
        - verse: "During the night Pharaoh summoned Moses and Aaron and said, \u201CUp!
            Leave my people, you and the Israelites! Go, worship the Lord as you
            have requested."
        - verse: "Take your flocks and herds, as you have said, and go. And also
            bless me.\u201D"
        - verse: "The Egyptians urged the people to hurry and leave the country.
            \u201CFor otherwise,\u201D they said, \u201Cwe will all die!\u201D"
      - pageID: 22023
        verses:
        - verse: So the people took their dough before the yeast was added, and carried
            it on their shoulders in kneading troughs wrapped in clothing.
        - verse: The Israelites did as Moses instructed and asked the Egyptians for
            articles of silver and gold and for clothing.
      - pageID: 32023
        verses:
        - verse: The Lord had made the Egyptians favorably disposed toward the people,
            and they gave them what they asked for; so they plundered the Egyptians.
        - verse: The Israelites journeyed from Rameses to Sukkoth. There were about
            six hundred thousand men on foot, besides women and children.
        - verse: Many other people went up with them, and also large droves of livestock,
            both flocks and herds.
      - pageID: 42023
        verses:
        - verse: With the dough the Israelites had brought from Egypt, they baked
            loaves of unleavened bread. The dough was without yeast because they
            had been driven out of Egypt and did not have time to prepare food for
            themselves.
        - verse: Now the length of time the Israelite people lived in Egypt was 430
            years.
        - verse: "At the end of the 430 years, to the very day, all the Lord\u2019s
            divisions left Egypt."
      - pageID: 52023
        verses:
        - verse: Because the Lord kept vigil that night to bring them out of Egypt,
            on this night all the Israelites are to keep vigil to honor the Lord
            for the generations to come.
    - passageID: 2024
      title: Crossing the Sea
      pages:
      - pageID: 12024
        verses:
        - verse: "When Pharaoh let the people go, God did not lead them on the road
            through the Philistine country, though that was shorter. For God said,
            \u201CIf they face war, they might change their minds and return to Egypt.\u201D"
        - verse: So God led the people around by the desert road toward the Red Sea.[a]
            The Israelites went up out of Egypt ready for battle.
      - pageID: 22024
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses took the bones of Joseph with him because Joseph had made
            the Israelites swear an oath. He had said, \u201CGod will surely come
            to your aid, and then you must carry my bones up with you from this place.\u201D"
        - verse: After leaving Sukkoth they camped at Etham on the edge of the desert.
      - pageID: 32024
        verses:
        - verse: By day the Lord went ahead of them in a pillar of cloud to guide
            them on their way and by night in a pillar of fire to give them light,
            so that they could travel by day or night.
        - verse: Neither the pillar of cloud by day nor the pillar of fire by night
            left its place in front of the people.
        - verse: Then the Lord said to Moses,
      - pageID: 42024
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CTell the Israelites to turn back and encamp near Pi Hahiroth,
            between Migdol and the sea. They are to encamp by the sea, directly opposite
            Baal Zephon."
        - verse: "Pharaoh will think, \u2018The Israelites are wandering around the
            land in confusion, hemmed in by the desert.\u2019"
        - verse: "And I will harden Pharaoh\u2019s heart, and he will pursue them.
            But I will gain glory for myself through Pharaoh and all his army, and
            the Egyptians will know that I am the Lord.\u201D So the Israelites did
            this."
      - pageID: 52024
        verses:
        - verse: "When the king of Egypt was told that the people had fled, Pharaoh
            and his officials changed their minds about them and said, \u201CWhat
            have we done? We have let the Israelites go and have lost their services!\u201D"
        - verse: So he had his chariot made ready and took his army with him.
      - pageID: 62024
        verses:
        - verse: He took six hundred of the best chariots, along with all the other
            chariots of Egypt, with officers over all of them.
        - verse: The Lord hardened the heart of Pharaoh king of Egypt, so that he
            pursued the Israelites, who were marching out boldly.
      - pageID: 72024
        verses:
        - verse: "The Egyptians\u2014all Pharaoh\u2019s horses and chariots, horsemen
            and troops\u2014pursued the Israelites and overtook them as they camped
            by the sea near Pi Hahiroth, opposite Baal Zephon."
        - verse: As Pharaoh approached, the Israelites looked up, and there were
            the Egyptians, marching after them. They were terrified and cried out
            to the Lord.
      - pageID: 82024
        verses:
        - verse: "They said to Moses, \u201CWas it because there were no graves in
            Egypt that you brought us to the desert to die? What have you done to
            us by bringing us out of Egypt?"
        - verse: "Didn\u2019t we say to you in Egypt, \u2018Leave us alone; let us
            serve the Egyptians\u2019? It would have been better for us to serve
            the Egyptians than to die in the desert!\u201D"
      - pageID: 92024
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses answered the people, \u201CDo not be afraid. Stand firm and
            you will see the deliverance the Lord will bring you today. The Egyptians
            you see today you will never see again."
        - verse: "The Lord will fight for you; you need only to be still.\u201D"
      - pageID: 102024
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CWhy are you crying out to me?
            Tell the Israelites to move on."
        - verse: Raise your staff and stretch out your hand over the sea to divide
            the water so that the Israelites can go through the sea on dry ground.
      - pageID: 112024
        verses:
        - verse: I will harden the hearts of the Egyptians so that they will go in
            after them. And I will gain glory through Pharaoh and all his army, through
            his chariots and his horsemen.
        - verse: "The Egyptians will know that I am the Lord when I gain glory through
            Pharaoh, his chariots and his horsemen.\u201D"
      - pageID: 122024
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the angel of God, who had been traveling in front of Israel\u2019s
            army, withdrew and went behind them. The pillar of cloud also moved from
            in front and stood behind them,"
        - verse: coming between the armies of Egypt and Israel. Throughout the night
            the cloud brought darkness to the one side and light to the other side;
            so neither went near the other all night long.
      - pageID: 132024
        verses:
        - verse: Then Moses stretched out his hand over the sea, and all that night
            the Lord drove the sea back with a strong east wind and turned it into
            dry land. The waters were divided,
        - verse: and the Israelites went through the sea on dry ground, with a wall
            of water on their right and on their left.
      - pageID: 142024
        verses:
        - verse: "The Egyptians pursued them, and all Pharaoh\u2019s horses and chariots
            and horsemen followed them into the sea."
        - verse: During the last watch of the night the Lord looked down from the
            pillar of fire and cloud at the Egyptian army and threw it into confusion.
      - pageID: 152024
        verses:
        - verse: "He jammed the wheels of their chariots so that they had difficulty
            driving. And the Egyptians said, \u201CLet\u2019s get away from the Israelites!
            The Lord is fighting for them against Egypt.\u201D"
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CStretch out your hand over the
            sea so that the waters may flow back over the Egyptians and their chariots
            and horsemen.\u201D"
      - pageID: 162024
        verses:
        - verse: Moses stretched out his hand over the sea, and at daybreak the sea
            went back to its place. The Egyptians were fleeing toward it, and the
            Lord swept them into the sea.
        - verse: "The water flowed back and covered the chariots and horsemen\u2014the
            entire army of Pharaoh that had followed the Israelites into the sea.
            Not one of them survived."
        - verse: But the Israelites went through the sea on dry ground, with a wall
            of water on their right and on their left.
      - pageID: 172024
        verses:
        - verse: That day the Lord saved Israel from the hands of the Egyptians,
            and Israel saw the Egyptians lying dead on the shore.
        - verse: And when the Israelites saw the mighty hand of the Lord displayed
            against the Egyptians, the people feared the Lord and put their trust
            in him and in Moses his servant.
    - passageID: 2025
      title: At Mount Sinai
      pages:
      - pageID: 12025
        verses:
        - verse: "On the first day of the third month after the Israelites left Egypt\u2014on
            that very day\u2014they came to the Desert of Sinai."
      - pageID: 22025
        verses:
        - verse: After they set out from Rephidim, they entered the Desert of Sinai,
            and Israel camped there in the desert in front of the mountain.
        - verse: "Then Moses went up to God, and the Lord called to him from the
            mountain and said, \u201CThis is what you are to say to the descendants
            of Jacob and what you are to tell the people of Israel:"
      - pageID: 32025
        verses:
        - verse: "You yourselves have seen what I did to Egypt, and how I carried
            you on eagles\u2019 wings and brought you to myself. "
        - verse: Now if you obey me fully and keep my covenant, then out of all nations
            you will be my treasured possession. Although the whole earth is mine,
        - verse: "you will be for me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.\u2019
            These are the words you are to speak to the Israelites.\u201D"
      - pageID: 42025
        verses:
        - verse: So Moses went back and summoned the elders of the people and set
            before them all the words the Lord had commanded him to speak.
        - verse: "The people all responded together, \u201CWe will do everything
            the Lord has said.\u201D So Moses brought their answer back to the Lord."
      - pageID: 52025
        verses:
        - verse: "The Lord said to Moses, \u201CI am going to come to you in a dense
            cloud, so that the people will hear me speaking with you and will always
            put their trust in you.\u201D Then Moses told the Lord what the people
            had said."
        - verse: "And the Lord said to Moses, \u201CGo to the people and consecrate
            them today and tomorrow. Have them wash their clothes"
        - verse: and be ready by the third day, because on that day the Lord will
            come down on Mount Sinai in the sight of all the people.
      - pageID: 62025
        verses:
        - verse: "Put limits for the people around the mountain and tell them, \u2018Be
            careful that you do not approach the mountain or touch the foot of it.
            Whoever touches the mountain is to be put to death."
        - verse: "They are to be stoned or shot with arrows; not a hand is to be
            laid on them. No person or animal shall be permitted to live.\u2019 Only
            when the ram\u2019s horn sounds a long blast may they approach the mountain.\u201D"
      - pageID: 72025
        verses:
        - verse: After Moses had gone down the mountain to the people, he consecrated
            them, and they washed their clothes.
        - verse: "Then he said to the people, \u201CPrepare yourselves for the third
            day. Abstain from sexual relations.\u201D"
      - pageID: 82025
        verses:
        - verse: On the morning of the third day there was thunder and lightning,
            with a thick cloud over the mountain, and a very loud trumpet blast.
            Everyone in the camp trembled.
        - verse: Then Moses led the people out of the camp to meet with God, and
            they stood at the foot of the mountain.
      - pageID: 92025
        verses:
        - verse: Mount Sinai was covered with smoke, because the Lord descended on
            it in fire. The smoke billowed up from it like smoke from a furnace,
            and the whole mountain trembled violently.
        - verse: As the sound of the trumpet grew louder and louder, Moses spoke
            and the voice of God answered him.
        - verse: The Lord descended to the top of Mount Sinai and called Moses to
            the top of the mountain. So Moses went up
      - pageID: 102025
        verses:
        - verse: "and the Lord said to him, \u201CGo down and warn the people so
            they do not force their way through to see the Lord and many of them
            perish."
        - verse: "Even the priests, who approach the Lord, must consecrate themselves,
            or the Lord will break out against them.\u201D"
      - pageID: 112025
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses said to the Lord, \u201CThe people cannot come up Mount Sinai,
            because you yourself warned us, \u2018Put limits around the mountain
            and set it apart as holy.\u2019\u201D"
        - verse: "The Lord replied, \u201CGo down and bring Aaron up with you. But
            the priests and the people must not force their way through to come up
            to the Lord, or he will break out against them.\u201D"
        - verse: So Moses went down to the people and told them.
    - passageID: 2026
      title: The Ten Commandements
      pages:
      - pageID: 12026
        verses:
        - verse: 'And God spoke all these words:'
        - verse: "\u201CI am the Lord your God, who brought you out of Egypt, out
            of the land of slavery."
        - verse: "\u201CYou shall have no other gods before me."
      - pageID: 22026
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CYou shall not make for yourself an image in the form of anything
            in heaven above or on the earth beneath or in the waters below."
        - verse: You shall not bow down to them or worship them; for I, the Lord
            your God, am a jealous God, punishing the children for the sin of the
            parents to the third and fourth generation of those who hate me,
        - verse: but showing love to a thousand generations of those who love me
            and keep my commandments.
      - pageID: 32026
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CYou shall not misuse the name of the Lord your God, for the
            Lord will not hold anyone guiltless who misuses his name."
        - verse: "\u201CRemember the Sabbath day by keeping it holy"
        - verse: Six days you shall labor and do all your work,
      - pageID: 42026
        verses:
        - verse: but the seventh day is a sabbath to the Lord your God. On it you
            shall not do any work, neither you, nor your son or daughter, nor your
            male or female servant, nor your animals, nor any foreigner residing
            in your towns.
        - verse: For in six days the Lord made the heavens and the earth, the sea,
            and all that is in them, but he rested on the seventh day. Therefore
            the Lord blessed the Sabbath day and made it holy.
      - pageID: 52026
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CHonor your father and your mother, so that you may live long
            in the land the Lord your God is giving you."
        - verse: "\u201CYou shall not murder."
        - verse: "\u201CYou shall not commit adultery."
        - verse: "\u201CYou shall not steal."
        - verse: "\u201CYou shall not give false testimony against your neighbor."
      - pageID: 62026
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201CYou shall not covet your neighbor\u2019s house. You shall
            not covet your neighbor\u2019s wife, or his male or female servant, his
            ox or donkey, or anything that belongs to your neighbor.\u201D"
      - pageID: 72026
        verses:
        - verse: When the people saw the thunder and lightning and heard the trumpet
            and saw the mountain in smoke, they trembled with fear. They stayed at
            a distance
        - verse: "and said to Moses, \u201CSpeak to us yourself and we will listen.
            But do not have God speak to us or we will die.\u201D"
      - pageID: 82026
        verses:
        - verse: "Moses said to the people, \u201CDo not be afraid. God has come
            to test you, so that the fear of God will be with you to keep you from
            sinning.\u201D"
        - verse: The people remained at a distance, while Moses approached the thick
            darkness where God was.
    - passageID: 2027
      title: Idols and Altars
      pages:
      - pageID: 12027
        verses:
        - verse: "Then the Lord said to Moses, \u201CTell the Israelites this: \u2018You
            have seen for yourselves that I have spoken to you from heaven:"
        - verse: Do not make any gods to be alongside me; do not make for yourselves
            gods of silver or gods of gold.
      - pageID: 22027
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Make an altar of earth for me and sacrifice on it your
            burnt offerings and fellowship offerings, your sheep and goats and your
            cattle. Wherever I cause my name to be honored, I will come to you and
            bless you."
        - verse: If you make an altar of stones for me, do not build it with dressed
            stones, for you will defile it if you use a tool on it.
        - verse: "And do not go up to my altar on steps, or your private parts may
            be exposed.\u2019"
  - bookID: 3
    book: Leviticus
    passages:
    - passageID: 3001
      title: Various Laws
      pages:
      - pageID: 13001
        verses:
        - verse: The Lord said to Moses,
        - verse: "\u201CSpeak to the entire assembly of Israel and say to them: \u2018Be
            holy because I, the Lord your God, am holy."
      - pageID: 23001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Each of you must respect your mother and father, and
            you must observe my Sabbaths. I am the Lord your God."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not turn to idols or make metal gods for yourselves.
            I am the Lord your God."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018When you sacrifice a fellowship offering to the Lord,
            sacrifice it in such a way that it will be accepted on your behalf."
      - pageID: 33001
        verses:
        - verse: ' It shall be eaten on the day you sacrifice it or on the next day;
            anything left over until the third day must be burned up.'
        - verse: If any of it is eaten on the third day, it is impure and will not
            be accepted.
      - pageID: 43001
        verses:
        - verse: Whoever eats it will be held responsible because they have desecrated
            what is holy to the Lord; they must be cut off from their people.
      - pageID: 53001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018When you reap the harvest of your land, do not reap
            to the very edges of your field or gather the gleanings of your harvest."
      - pageID: 63001
        verses:
        - verse: Do not go over your vineyard a second time or pick up the grapes
            that have fallen. Leave them for the poor and the foreigner. I am the
            Lord your God.
      - pageID: 73001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not steal.\r\n\r\n\u201C\u2018Do not lie.\r\n\r\n\u201C\u2018Do
            not deceive one another."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not swear falsely by my name and so profane the
            name of your God. I am the Lord."
      - pageID: 83001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not defraud or rob your neighbor.\r\n\r\n\u201C\u2018Do
            not hold back the wages of a hired worker overnight."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not curse the deaf or put a stumbling block in front
            of the blind, but fear your God. I am the Lord."
      - pageID: 93001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not pervert justice; do not show partiality to the
            poor or favoritism to the great, but judge your neighbor fairly."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not go about spreading slander among your people.\n\n\u201C\u2018Do
            not do anything that endangers your neighbor\u2019s life. I am the Lord."
      - pageID: 103001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not hate a fellow Israelite in your heart. Rebuke
            your neighbor frankly so you will not share in their guilt."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not seek revenge or bear a grudge against anyone
            among your people, but love your neighbor as yourself. I am the Lord."
      - pageID: 113001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Keep my decrees.\r\n\r\n\u201C\u2018Do not mate different
            kinds of animals.\r\n\r\n\u201C\u2018Do not plant your field with two
            kinds of seed.\r\n\r\n\u201C\u2018Do not wear clothing woven of two kinds
            of material."
      - pageID: 123001
        verses:
        - verse: " \u201C\u2018If a man sleeps with a female slave who is promised
            to another man but who has not been ransomed or given her freedom, there
            must be due punishment.[a] Yet they are not to be put to death, because
            she had not been freed."
        - verse: The man, however, must bring a ram to the entrance to the tent of
            meeting for a guilt offering to the Lord.
      - pageID: 133001
        verses:
        - verse: With the ram of the guilt offering the priest is to make atonement
            for him before the Lord for the sin he has committed, and his sin will
            be forgiven.
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018When you enter the land and plant any kind of fruit
            tree, regard its fruit as forbidden.[b] For three years you are to consider
            it forbidden; it must not be eaten."
      - pageID: 143001
        verses:
        - verse: In the fourth year all its fruit will be holy, an offering of praise
            to the Lord.
        - verse: But in the fifth year you may eat its fruit. In this way your harvest
            will be increased. I am the Lord your God.
      - pageID: 153001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not eat any meat with the blood still in it.\r\n\r\n\u201C\u2018Do
            not practice divination or seek omens."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not cut the hair at the sides of your head or clip
            off the edges of your beard."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not cut your bodies for the dead or put tattoo marks
            on yourselves. I am the Lord."
      - pageID: 163001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not degrade your daughter by making her a prostitute,
            or the land will turn to prostitution and be filled with wickedness."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Observe my Sabbaths and have reverence for my sanctuary.
            I am the Lord."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not turn to mediums or seek out spiritists, for
            you will be defiled by them. I am the Lord your God."
      - pageID: 173001
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Stand up in the presence of the aged, show respect
            for the elderly and revere your God. I am the Lord."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018When a foreigner resides among you in your land, do
            not mistreat them."
      - pageID: 183001
        verses:
        - verse: The foreigner residing among you must be treated as your native-born.
            Love them as yourself, for you were foreigners in Egypt. I am the Lord
            your God.
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not use dishonest standards when measuring length,
            weight or quantity."
      - pageID: 193001
        verses:
        - verse: Use honest scales and honest weights, an honest ephah and an honest
            hin. I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of Egypt.
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Keep all my decrees and all my laws and follow them.
            I am the Lord.\u2019\u201D"
    - passageID: 3002
      title: Punishment for Sin
      pages:
      - pageID: 13002
        verses:
        - verse: The Lord said to Moses,
        - verse: "\u201CSay to the Israelites: \u2018Any Israelite or any foreigner
            residing in Israel who sacrifices any of his children to Molek is to
            be put to death. The members of the community are to stone him."
      - pageID: 23002
        verses:
        - verse: I myself will set my face against him and will cut him off from
            his people; for by sacrificing his children to Molek, he has defiled
            my sanctuary and profaned my holy name.
        - verse: If the members of the community close their eyes when that man sacrifices
            one of his children to Molek and if they fail to put him to death,
      - pageID: 33002
        verses:
        - verse: I myself will set my face against him and his family and will cut
            them off from their people together with all who follow him in prostituting
            themselves to Molek.
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018I will set my face against anyone who turns to mediums
            and spiritists to prostitute themselves by following them, and I will
            cut them off from their people."
      - pageID: 43002
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Consecrate yourselves and be holy, because I am the
            Lord your God."
        - verse: Keep my decrees and follow them. I am the Lord, who makes you holy.
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Anyone who curses their father or mother is to be put
            to death. Because they have cursed their father or mother, their blood
            will be on their own head."
      - pageID: 53002
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man commits adultery with another man\u2019s wife\u2014with
            the wife of his neighbor\u2014both the adulterer and the adulteress are
            to be put to death."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man has sexual relations with his father\u2019s
            wife, he has dishonored his father. Both the man and the woman are to
            be put to death; their blood will be on their own heads."
      - pageID: 63002
        verses:
        - verse: " \u201C\u2018If a man has sexual relations with his daughter-in-law,
            both of them are to be put to death. What they have done is a perversion;
            their blood will be on their own heads."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man has sexual relations with a man as one does
            with a woman, both of them have done what is detestable. They are to
            be put to death; their blood will be on their own heads."
      - pageID: 73002
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man marries both a woman and her mother, it is
            wicked. Both he and they must be burned in the fire, so that no wickedness
            will be among you."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man has sexual relations with an animal, he is
            to be put to death, and you must kill the animal."
      - pageID: 83002
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a woman approaches an animal to have sexual relations
            with it, kill both the woman and the animal. They are to be put to death;
            their blood will be on their own heads."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man marries his sister, the daughter of either
            his father or his mother, and they have sexual relations, it is a disgrace.
            They are to be publicly removed from their people. He has dishonored
            his sister and will be held responsible."
      - pageID: 93002
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man has sexual relations with a woman during her
            monthly period, he has exposed the source of her flow, and she has also
            uncovered it. Both of them are to be cut off from their people."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Do not have sexual relations with the sister of either
            your mother or your father, for that would dishonor a close relative;
            both of you would be held responsible."
      - pageID: 103002
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man has sexual relations with his aunt, he has
            dishonored his uncle. They will be held responsible; they will die childless."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018If a man marries his brother\u2019s wife, it is an
            act of impurity; he has dishonored his brother. They will be childless."
      - pageID: 113002
        verses:
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018Keep all my decrees and laws and follow them, so that
            the land where I am bringing you to live may not vomit you out."
        - verse: You must not live according to the customs of the nations I am going
            to drive out before you. Because they did all these things, I abhorred
            them.
      - pageID: 123002
        verses:
        - verse: "But I said to you, \u201CYou will possess their land; I will give
            it to you as an inheritance, a land flowing with milk and honey.\u201D
            I am the Lord your God, who has set you apart from the nations."
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018You must therefore make a distinction between clean
            and unclean animals and between unclean and clean birds. Do not defile
            yourselves by any animal or bird or anything that moves along the ground\u2014those
            that I have set apart as unclean for you."
      - pageID: 133002
        verses:
        - verse: You are to be holy to me because I, the Lord, am holy, and I have
            set you apart from the nations to be my own.
        - verse: "\u201C\u2018A man or woman who is a medium or spiritist among you
            must be put to death. You are to stone them; their blood will be on their
            own heads.\u2019\u201D"
